enum Admin2StudyBank {
    static let sections: [StudySection] = [
        // Security and Access (4 objectives, 37 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "security_access",
            title: "Security and Access",
            color: Color(red: 0.30, green: 0.50, blue: 0.80),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.94, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "lock.shield.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "security_access_obj1",
                    title: "Given a scenario, determine the implications to record and field data access (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q1", number: 1, question: "How OWD settings interact with role hierarchy grant-access-via-hierarchy to determine the most permissive effective access a user has on a record when both mechanisms apply", answer: "Salesforce uses a most-permissive wins model when combining access mechanisms. OWD sets the baseline (the floor). The role hierarchy can only open access further upward, never restrict it. If a user's OWD grants Read Only but their position in the role hierarchy above the record owner grants Read/Write, the user gets Read/Write. Effective access is the highest level granted by any single mechanism: OWD, role hierarchy, sharing rules, manual shares, or territory. None of these can take away access already granted by another."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q2", number: 2, question: "Sharing rules can only open access beyond OWD — they can never restrict access that has already been granted by OWD or another mechanism; understanding this unidirectional nature when diagnosing unexpected access", answer: "Sharing rules are additive only. If a user already has Read/Write via OWD or role hierarchy, a sharing rule granting Read Only to that same user has no effect — it cannot reduce their access. When diagnosing a case where a user has \"too much\" access, sharing rules are never the culprit. The problem will always be OWD being too open, or the role hierarchy or another grant providing broader access than intended."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q3", number: 3, question: "Criteria-based sharing rules vs. owner-based sharing rules: which triggers automatically when record data changes vs. when record ownership changes", answer: "- Owner-based sharing rules fire automatically when a record's owner changes. They share records owned by members of one group with members of another group.\n\n- Criteria-based sharing rules fire automatically when a record's field values change and match the rule's criteria (e.g., Industry = \"Technology\"). If a field value changes so the record now meets the criteria, the share is granted. If it no longer meets criteria, the share is revoked.\n\nKey distinction: criteria-based rules respond to data; owner-based rules respond to ownership."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q4", number: 4, question: "Public Groups in sharing rules: how a public group can include users, roles, roles and subordinates, and other public groups, and why using a public group in a sharing rule is more maintainable than adding individual users directly", answer: "A Public Group can contain any mix of: individual users, roles, roles and subordinates, and other public groups. When used as the target of a sharing rule, adding or removing a user from the group automatically updates their record access without touching the sharing rule itself. This is far more maintainable than building sharing rules around individual users, which would require rule changes every time someone joins or leaves a team."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q5", number: 5, question: "Field-Level Security vs. page layout visibility: which one is the authoritative security control that prevents a user from reading a field value via API", answer: "Field-Level Security (FLS) is the authoritative control. If a field is set to Hidden or Read Only via FLS on a profile or permission set, the user cannot see or access that field value through the UI, API, or reports — regardless of whether the field appears on a page layout. Page layout visibility only controls what is shown on the screen in the UI. A field hidden on a page layout but not restricted by FLS is still fully accessible via API. For true security, FLS must be configured."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q6", number: 6, question: "When a record's OWD is set to 'Controlled by Parent' on a child object and the parent is Private, determining what access a user without an explicit share on the parent record has to the child record", answer: "\"Controlled by Parent\" means the child record's access is entirely determined by the user's access to the parent record. If the parent object's OWD is Private and a user has no share on the parent, they have no access to the parent record. As a result, they also have no access to any child records tied to that parent, even if they know the child record exists. Access to the child requires access to the parent first."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q7", number: 7, question: "Grant Access Using Hierarchies setting on custom objects: how disabling this setting prevents the role hierarchy from granting access upward and the implications for manager access to subordinates' records", answer: "By default, users above a record owner in the role hierarchy inherit at least the same access the owner has. On custom objects, an admin can disable \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies.\" When disabled, the role hierarchy is no longer used to grant access for that object — a manager will not automatically see their subordinate's records. The only ways to access those records are via OWD (if not Private), sharing rules, or explicit manual shares. This setting cannot be disabled on standard objects."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q8", number: 8, question: "Manual sharing behavior on ownership transfer: when a record owner changes, which sharing grants are automatically removed", answer: "When a record's owner changes, all manual shares (shares created via the Sharing button) on that record are automatically deleted. Shares granted via OWD, role hierarchy, sharing rules, or territory management are unaffected and recalculated based on the new owner. The practical implication: if a user manually shared a record with someone, and ownership is then transferred, that manual share is gone and must be re-created if still needed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q9", number: 9, question: "Report folder and email template folder sharing: which user types (public groups, roles, roles and subordinates) can be granted access", answer: "Report and email template folders can be shared with: individual users, public groups, roles, and roles and subordinates. Access levels for report folders are: Viewer (can run), Editor (can run and save), and Manager (can manage the folder). Sharing folders with roles and subordinates is especially useful for giving an entire sales hierarchy access to a set of reports without managing individual users. Guest users cannot access shared folders."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q10", number: 10, question: "Dashboard folder sharing: the access levels available (Viewer, Editor, Manager) and which level allows a user to save a dashboard back to a shared folder", answer: "Dashboard folders have three access levels:\n\n- Viewer: Can view and run dashboards in the folder, but cannot save changes.\n\n- Editor: Can view, run, and edit dashboards, and save them back to the folder.\n\n- Manager: Can do everything Editors can, plus manage folder membership and delete the folder.\n\nTo save a modified dashboard back to a shared folder, a user needs at minimum Editor access on that folder."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q11", number: 11, question: "Territory Management 2.0 model states (Planning, Active, Archived) and what actions are allowed in each state", answer: "| State | What You Can Do |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| Planning | Create/edit territory types, models, hierarchy, and rules. Cannot run assignment rules org-wide. |\n\n| Active | Run assignment rules, associate users to territories, connect to forecasting. Only one model can be Active at a time. |\n\n| Archived | Read-only. No edits, no rule execution. Used for historical reference. |\n\nYou must activate a model before its territories affect record access or forecasting."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q12", number: 12, question: "How Territory Management 2.0 assignment rules determine which territories are assigned to an account and how that propagates access to related opportunities and contacts", answer: "Territory assignment rules evaluate account field values (e.g., State, Industry, Annual Revenue) and assign matching accounts to territories. When an account is saved, Salesforce runs all active rules in the active model and assigns the account to every matching territory. Users assigned to those territories then gain access to the account. Opportunities associated with the account are also accessible to territory users. Contacts associated with the account gain the same territory-based access through the account relationship."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q13", number: 13, question: "Given a multi-mechanism scenario (Private OWD + role hierarchy + criteria-based sharing rule + Territory Management), identifying which mechanism grants a specific user Read/Write access to an account record", answer: "Work through each mechanism in turn and take the highest access granted:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q14", number: 14, question: "**OWD Private**: grants nothing by default.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q15", number: 15, question: "**Role Hierarchy**: does the user sit above the record owner? If yes, grants at least the owner's access.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q16", number: 16, question: "**Criteria-based sharing rule**: does the account's field data match the rule criteria and is the user in the target group? Grants the access level defined on the rule.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj1_q17", number: 17, question: "**Territory Management**: is the user assigned to a territory that covers this account? Grants access per territory settings.", answer: "The user's effective access is the highest level from any single mechanism. To determine *which* one granted it, identify the first mechanism that produces the access level in question.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "security_access_obj2",
                    title: "Compare and contrast the capabilities of custom profiles, permission sets, and delegated administration.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj2_q1", number: 18, question: "Permission sets vs. profiles: which object-level permissions can be granted via permission set but NOT removed via permission set", answer: "Permission sets can only add permissions on top of what a profile grants — they cannot take permissions away. For example, if a user's profile grants Read on Accounts, a permission set cannot remove that Read access. The only way to remove an object permission is to edit the profile directly. This means profiles still define the minimum permission baseline, and permission sets are purely additive grants layered on top."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj2_q2", number: 19, question: "Custom profiles: limitations on deletion (cannot delete a profile assigned to a user) and cloning behavior when the source profile has a permission not available in the target edition", answer: "You cannot delete a custom profile if any user is currently assigned to it. You must first reassign all those users to a different profile. When cloning a profile, if the source profile contains permissions that are not available in the current edition (e.g., a feature only in Enterprise), those permissions are silently dropped from the clone. Always review the cloned profile to confirm all expected permissions carried over correctly."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj2_q3", number: 20, question: "Delegated administration: what a delegated administrator CAN do (manage users in specified roles/subordinates, assign specified profiles and permission sets, unlock users) vs. what they CANNOT do (change OWDs, create or clone profiles)", answer: "Can do:\n\n- Create and edit users in specified roles and their subordinates.\n\n- Assign only the profiles and permission sets explicitly listed in their delegation group.\n\n- Unlock user accounts and reset passwords for users in their scope.\n\nCannot do:\n\n- Change Organization-Wide Defaults.\n\n- Create, clone, or delete profiles.\n\n- Manage users outside their delegated scope.\n\n- Change system-level settings or org security.\n\nDelegated admins are scoped helpers, not full system administrators."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj2_q4", number: 21, question: "Delegated administrator scope: a delegated admin is restricted to managing users within which roles relative to their defined delegation group", answer: "A delegated administrator can manage users in the roles explicitly listed in their delegation group AND all subordinate roles below those roles in the hierarchy. They cannot manage peers, users in roles above their scope, or users in entirely separate role branches. The scope is always downward from the specified roles."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj2_q5", number: 22, question: "Permission set groups: how stacking multiple permission sets into a group differs from assigning each permission set individually", answer: "A Permission Set Group bundles multiple permission sets into a single unit for assignment. The result is the union of all permissions across every permission set in the group. Functionally, assigning a group or assigning each constituent permission set individually produces the same net permissions. The advantage of a group is operational: you assign one thing to a user instead of many, and adding or removing a permission set from the group instantly updates all users assigned to the group."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj2_q6", number: 23, question: "Permission set groups: the role of muting permission sets within a group and when muting is appropriate", answer: "A Muting Permission Set is a special permission set that can only exist inside a Permission Set Group. It selectively removes specific permissions that one of the group's constituent permission sets would otherwise grant. Use muting when you want to use a broad permission set (e.g., \"Sales Rep Full Access\") but need to suppress one specific permission (e.g., Delete on Opportunities) without creating an entirely new permission set. Muting avoids the overhead of maintaining duplicated permission sets with minor variations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj2_q7", number: 24, question: "Permission Set License (PSL): the concept of a license-based permission set that grants access to features available only in add-on licenses (e.g., Einstein Analytics, Salesforce Maps) and how PSLs differ from standard permission sets", answer: "A Permission Set License (PSL) is a license entitlement that must be assigned to a user before any permission set granting the related feature can be assigned. For example, to grant a user access to Einstein Analytics, they must first have the Einstein Analytics PSL assigned, and then a permission set enabling specific Analytics features. PSLs are tied to purchased add-on licenses — they are not configuration objects you create. Standard permission sets grant feature access within the org's existing license; PSLs unlock entirely separate licensed feature sets.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "security_access_obj3",
                    title: "Given a scenario, determine the implications of user authentication.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj3_q1", number: 25, question: "My Domain requirement: which Salesforce features require My Domain to be enabled before they can be configured (SSO, Lightning components, etc.)", answer: "My Domain must be enabled before you can configure:\n\n- Single Sign-On (SSO) with external identity providers.\n\n- Lightning components (Aura and LWC) deployed to the org.\n\n- Social Sign-On (login via Google, Facebook, etc.).\n\n- Canvas apps.\n\n- Named Credentials that reference the org's domain.\n\nMy Domain gives the org a unique URL (e.g., `yourcompany.my.salesforce.com`), which is required for these features to function correctly and securely."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj3_q2", number: 26, question: "Login IP Ranges (on profiles) vs. Trusted IP Ranges (org-wide): which setting prevents login entirely vs. which triggers an email verification challenge when outside the range", answer: "- Login IP Ranges (profile-level): If a user's IP is outside the configured range, login is completely blocked. No workaround via email challenge. The user simply cannot log in.\n\n- Trusted IP Ranges (org-wide, in Security Controls): If a user's IP is outside this range, Salesforce sends an email verification challenge (an activation link). The user can still log in after clicking the link. This is a friction-add, not a hard block.\n\nThe profile-level setting is the stricter enforcement mechanism."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj3_q3", number: 27, question: "Login Hours: how the profile-level restriction blocks access outside configured hours and how it differs from IP-based restrictions", answer: "Login Hours restrict when users on a specific profile can log in. Outside configured hours, login is blocked entirely. If a user is already logged in when their allowed window closes, their session expires and they are logged out at the next page load. Unlike IP restrictions, Login Hours are time-based only and have nothing to do with network location. Both restrictions can apply simultaneously to the same profile — a user must be in both the right IP range and within the allowed hours to log in."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj3_q4", number: 28, question: "Session Settings: session timeout values and how idle sessions are terminated", answer: "Session Settings (under Security Controls) control how long an authenticated session remains valid. The timeout value defines the maximum idle time before a session is automatically invalidated (options range from 15 minutes to 8 hours, default is 2 hours). When the idle timeout is reached, the user's session is terminated and they are redirected to the login page on their next action. Admins can also set an absolute session timeout regardless of activity and enforce re-authentication after the session expires."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj3_q5", number: 29, question: "SAML-based SSO: the role of the Identity Provider (IdP), the Service Provider (SP), and the assertion consumer service URL in the login flow", answer: "- Identity Provider (IdP): The system that authenticates the user and issues the SAML assertion (e.g., Okta, Azure AD, ADFS). It is the \"trust source.\"\n\n- Service Provider (SP): Salesforce. It receives and validates the SAML assertion but relies on the IdP to do the actual authentication.\n\n- Assertion Consumer Service (ACS) URL: The Salesforce endpoint that receives the SAML response from the IdP after a successful authentication. The IdP posts the assertion to this URL to complete the login flow.\n\nFlow: User attempts to access Salesforce → redirected to IdP → user authenticates at IdP → IdP posts SAML assertion to ACS URL → Salesforce validates assertion → user is logged in."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj3_q6", number: 30, question: "MFA enforcement: the difference between enabling MFA in Session Settings vs. using a Permission Set vs. org-wide enforcement", answer: "- Session Settings (profile-level): Enables MFA for users on a specific profile. Granular control but requires per-profile configuration.\n\n- Permission Set: A \"Multi-Factor Authentication for User Interface Logins\" permission can be added to a permission set and assigned to specific users, regardless of their profile.\n\n- Org-wide enforcement: Via the \"Require MFA for All Direct UI Logins\" setting in Session Settings or by enabling the Salesforce MFA requirement, it applies to every user in the org with no exceptions at the profile level.\n\nOrg-wide enforcement is the most comprehensive and least granular. Salesforce requires MFA for all orgs."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj3_q7", number: 31, question: "MFA enforcement: what happens to existing user sessions when MFA is newly enabled or enforced", answer: "Existing active sessions are not immediately terminated when MFA is newly enabled. Users currently logged in can continue their session. However, the next time they log in (after their current session expires or they log out), they will be required to complete MFA. There is no forced re-authentication of live sessions at the moment MFA is switched on."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj3_q8", number: 32, question: "Connected App OAuth flow: when an admin must pre-authorize a connected app for all users vs. requiring user-level authorization", answer: "- Admin pre-authorization (\"Admin approved users are pre-authorized\"): The admin enables the connected app for all users and approves it centrally. Individual users are not prompted for OAuth consent. Use this for internal integrations where you do not want users to see or manage OAuth prompts.\n\n- User-level authorization (\"All users may self-authorize\"): Each user must individually approve the connected app's access to their account the first time they authenticate. Use this for apps where individual user consent is appropriate or required.\n\nThe Connected App's OAuth Policies setting controls this behavior.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "security_access_obj4",
                    title: "Compare and contrast the capabilities of a complex business model (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj4_q1", number: 33, question: "Person Accounts: the record types they merge (Account + Contact into one record) and which standard Contact features are unavailable (e.g., cannot relate a Person Account contact to multiple accounts)", answer: "A Person Account is a single record that combines Account and Contact fields, designed for B2C scenarios where your customers are individuals rather than businesses. Because the Contact is merged into the Account record, the following standard Contact features are not available for Person Accounts:\n\n- Cannot be related to multiple accounts (Contacts to Multiple Accounts).\n\n- Cannot have a Reports To relationship.\n\n- Cannot be the primary contact on a separate Account record.\n\n- Do not appear in the standard Contacts list view.\n\nPerson Accounts behave like Accounts in sharing and like Contacts in some Service Cloud features."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj4_q2", number: 34, question: "Person Accounts enablement: once Person Accounts are enabled, what change is irreversible and what prerequisite record type setup is required", answer: "Enabling Person Accounts is irreversible — you cannot disable it once turned on. Before enabling, Salesforce requires that at least one record type exists on both the Account and Contact objects. This is a hard prerequisite; the feature will not activate without it. Because it is permanent, Salesforce Support must assist with enablement. Plan carefully: existing data models, integrations, and AppExchange apps may behave differently after Person Accounts are enabled."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj4_q3", number: 35, question: "Person Accounts OWD implications: how the sharing model differs for Person Account records compared to standard Account-Contact relationships", answer: "Person Account records follow the Account OWD for sharing purposes, not the Contact OWD. In a standard Account-Contact model, Contact sharing is governed by the Contact OWD and the parent Account's sharing. With Person Accounts, there is no separate Contact record, so the Account sharing model entirely controls visibility. This simplifies sharing in some ways but means the Contact OWD setting has no effect on Person Account visibility."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj4_q4", number: 36, question: "Contacts to Multiple Accounts: how an indirect account–contact relationship differs from the direct (primary) relationship, and which account's sharing rules govern the contact's visibility", answer: "Every Contact has one primary (direct) Account, stored in the Account Name field, which governs the Contact's OWD-based access and whose sharing rules control visibility of the Contact. A Contact can also be indirectly related to other Accounts via the Account Contact Relationship object — these are secondary relationships. However, the sharing rules, OWD, and role hierarchy of indirect accounts do not grant access to the Contact. Only the primary account's sharing model does. Users who can see an indirect account do not automatically see the related contacts unless they can also see those contacts through the primary account path."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_access_obj4_q5", number: 37, question: "Given a scenario with both business accounts and person accounts in the same org, identifying which standard Salesforce features behave differently or are restricted for person account records", answer: "When both coexist in the same org:\n\n- Campaigns: Person Accounts can be added as campaign members; standard business account contacts can too, but you must handle them separately.\n\n- Cases: Person Accounts can be the contact on a case, but the relationship lookup behaves differently.\n\n- Duplicate Management: Matching rules must account for the fact that Person Accounts are queried differently from Contacts.\n\n- Reports: You must use specific Person Account report types, as standard Contact reports do not include Person Accounts.\n\n- Partner/Customer Community: Person Accounts can be enabled as community users, but certain portal features behave differently.\n\n- Contacts to Multiple Accounts: Unavailable for Person Accounts.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Objects and Applications (2 objectives, 26 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "objects_apps",
            title: "Objects and Applications",
            color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.60, blue: 0.50),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.96, blue: 0.94),
            icon: "square.stack.3d.up.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "objects_apps_obj1",
                    title: "Given a scenario, determine the appropriate solution to enhance or extend objects (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q1", number: 38, question: "Master-detail vs. lookup relationship: which relationship type rolls up summary fields, enforces cascade delete, and controls the child record's OWD", answer: "| Feature | Master-Detail | Lookup |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Roll-Up Summary Fields | Yes | No |\n\n| Cascade Delete | Yes (child deleted with parent) | No (child remains, field nulled or blocked) |\n\n| Child OWD | Controlled by Parent | Independent |\n\n| Required parent field | Yes | Optional |\n\n| Max per object | 2 | 25 |\n\nMaster-detail is a tighter, more dependent relationship. Lookup is looser and more flexible."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q2", number: 39, question: "Lookup filter on a lookup field: how a lookup filter restricts the records a user can select in a lookup and the difference between a required vs. optional lookup filter behavior", answer: "A lookup filter narrows the records available in the lookup search dialog based on field criteria (e.g., only show Accounts where Type = \"Customer\").\n\n- Required filter: The user cannot select or save a record that does not meet the filter criteria. An error is shown if they try.\n\n- Optional filter: The filter is applied by default to pre-filter results, but the user can click \"Search Without Filter\" to see all records and select one that does not match the criteria. No error is thrown on save."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q3", number: 40, question: "Junction objects: how to model a many-to-many relationship between two standard objects and the implications for OWD on the junction object", answer: "A junction object is a custom object with two master-detail relationships, one to each of the two objects in the many-to-many relationship (e.g., Campaign Member joining Campaign and Contact). The junction object's OWD is set to Controlled by Parent, meaning it inherits access from whichever parent gives the user the most access. There is no independent OWD for the junction record itself. Cascade delete applies from both parents: deleting either parent deletes the junction records related to it."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q4", number: 41, question: "Roll-up summary fields: which aggregate functions are supported (COUNT, SUM, MIN, MAX) and which child relationship types allow them", answer: "Supported aggregate functions: COUNT, SUM, MIN, MAX.\n\n- COUNT: Counts child records (any field type).\n\n- SUM: Sums a numeric field on child records.\n\n- MIN / MAX: Returns the minimum or maximum value of a numeric, currency, date, or date/time field on child records.\n\nRoll-up summary fields are only supported on the master object in a master-detail relationship. They cannot be created on lookup relationships."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q5", number: 42, question: "Roll-up summary fields: whether filter criteria can be applied to restrict which child records are included in the aggregate", answer: "Yes. When creating a roll-up summary field, you can optionally add filter criteria to restrict which child records are included in the calculation. For example, a SUM roll-up on Opportunity Amount could filter to only include child records where Stage = \"Closed Won.\" Child records that do not match the filter are excluded from the aggregate. This makes roll-up summaries very flexible for targeted calculations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q6", number: 43, question: "Converting a lookup relationship to master-detail: which pre-conditions must be met (all existing child records must have a parent value, no existing validation or default field rules on the field)", answer: "To convert a lookup to master-detail:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q7", number: 44, question: "**All existing child records must have a value in the lookup field** (no blank/null parent values allowed, since master-detail requires a parent).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q8", number: 45, question: "The lookup field cannot have any **default field values** set.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q9", number: 46, question: "The object cannot exceed the **limit of 2 master-detail relationships** per object.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q10", number: 47, question: "The relationship must not be **self-referential**.", answer: "If any child records have a null parent, the conversion will fail until those records are updated."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q11", number: 48, question: "Record types: how they control picklist values and page layout assignments, and the distinction between the Record Type and the page layout", answer: "A record type controls two things:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q12", number: 49, question: "**Picklist values**: Which values from a picklist field's global value set are available when a user with that record type creates or edits a record.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q13", number: 50, question: "**Page layout assignment**: Which page layout is shown to which profile when viewing a record of that type.", answer: "The record type and page layout are separate objects. The record type does not contain layout fields itself — it simply maps a profile to a layout. You can assign the same layout to multiple record types, or different layouts per profile within the same record type."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q14", number: 51, question: "Record types: how they control business process assignments (e.g., opportunity stages, case stages)", answer: "Record types are linked to Sales Processes (for Opportunities), Lead Processes (for Leads), and Support Processes (for Cases). These processes define which Stage or Status picklist values are available for that process. When a record type is tied to a specific process, only the stages in that process are available to users assigned that record type. This is how orgs can give different sales teams different sets of Opportunity stages using the same Opportunity object."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q15", number: 52, question: "Object Creator vs. Object Manager: when to use Object Creator for guided end-to-end custom object creation vs. Object Manager for modifying existing objects", answer: "- Object Creator: A guided, wizard-style tool for creating a brand-new custom object from scratch. It walks through naming, fields, relationships, and page layouts in a step-by-step flow. Best for first-time object creation.\n\n- Object Manager: The central hub for managing all objects (standard and custom). Use it to modify existing objects — adding fields, editing relationships, managing page layouts, record types, triggers, and more.\n\nObject Creator is creation-only. Object Manager is the ongoing management interface."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q16", number: 53, question: "Schema Builder: capabilities it provides beyond Object Manager (visualizing cross-object relationships, creating new fields and objects in a single canvas)", answer: "Schema Builder provides a visual, drag-and-drop canvas showing objects and their relationships as a connected diagram. Capabilities beyond Object Manager:\n\n- Visually see how multiple objects relate to each other at once.\n\n- Create new custom objects, fields, and relationships directly on the canvas without navigating away.\n\n- Drag fields from the palette onto objects on the canvas.\n\nIt is especially useful for understanding and designing complex data models with many cross-object relationships."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj1_q17", number: 54, question: "Schema Builder limitations: which operations it cannot perform (e.g., cannot delete objects or fields)", answer: "Schema Builder cannot:\n\n- Delete custom objects or fields.\n\n- Modify existing field properties in detail (e.g., formula syntax, complex field options).\n\n- Manage page layouts, record types, or validation rules.\n\n- Deploy or package metadata.\n\nFor deletion or detailed field editing, you must use Object Manager directly.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "objects_apps_obj2",
                    title: "Given a scenario, determine the appropriate solution to enhance or extend the UI/UX with applications (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj2_q1", number: 55, question: "App Manager: differences between a Classic app and a Lightning app — which settings are available only in Lightning apps (navigation items, utility bar, app options like 'Enable header' in console context)", answer: "| Feature | Classic App | Lightning App |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Navigation items | Basic tab bar | Dynamic navigation with standard & custom items |\n\n| Utility bar | No | Yes |\n\n| Console navigation | No | Yes (configurable) |\n\n| App branding (logo, color) | Limited | Full |\n\n| Form factor targeting | No | Yes (desktop, phone) |\n\nLightning apps also support the Service Console style with workspace tabs and split view. Classic apps are legacy and offer no utility bar or console configuration."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj2_q2", number: 56, question: "Lightning App Builder page types (App Page, Record Page, Home Page) and which assignment mechanisms (app, record type, profile) are available for each type", answer: "| Page Type | Assignment Options |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| App Page | Assign to specific Lightning apps |\n\n| Record Page | Assign by app, record type, and/or profile |\n\n| Home Page | Assign by app and/or profile |\n\nRecord Pages have the most granular assignment options, allowing you to tailor the layout per profile and record type combination. Home Pages can be app-specific or profile-specific. App Pages are simply added to one or more apps' navigation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj2_q3", number: 57, question: "Standard Lightning page components: which components are available on a standard Record Page by default vs. must be added from the component palette in Lightning App Builder", answer: "Available by default (already on the standard record page): Record Detail (fields), Related Lists, Chatter Feed, Activity Timeline, Highlights Panel (for standard objects).\n\nMust be added from the palette: Report Charts, Flow components, custom LWC components, Knowledge components, Recent Items, List Views, Milestones, and any third-party components from AppExchange.\n\nThe default page gives a functional layout; customization adds richness."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj2_q4", number: 58, question: "Component visibility filters in Lightning App Builder: how filter conditions (field values, record type, user permission, form factor) can conditionally show or hide a component on a Lightning record page without Dynamic Forms", answer: "Component Visibility Filters allow you to add one or more conditions to any component on a Lightning record page. The component only renders if all conditions are met. Filter types include:\n\n- Field value (e.g., show component only when Status = \"Open\").\n\n- Record type (show only for a specific record type).\n\n- User permission (show only if user has a specific permission).\n\n- Form factor (show only on desktop or mobile).\n\nThese filters work at the component level and do not require Dynamic Forms. Multiple conditions use AND logic by default."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj2_q5", number: 59, question: "Dynamic Forms vs. page layouts: how Dynamic Forms allows field-level and section-level visibility rules directly on the Lightning record page, replacing the need for multiple page layouts for the same record type", answer: "With traditional page layouts, field visibility is static per layout. To show different fields to different users or in different contexts, you need multiple layouts and profile/record type combinations.\n\nDynamic Forms moves individual fields and sections directly onto the Lightning record page as independent components. Each field or section can have its own visibility conditions (field value, user permission, profile, etc.). One record page with Dynamic Forms can replace many traditional page layouts, reducing layout sprawl significantly. This is only available in Lightning Experience."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj2_q6", number: 60, question: "Dynamic Forms eligibility: which object types support Dynamic Forms (custom objects and select standard objects in Lightning Experience) and which standard objects have limited or no Dynamic Forms support (e.g., Opportunity, Case, Lead, Contact may have limited support depending on release)", answer: "Dynamic Forms is fully supported for custom objects. Support for standard objects has been expanding with each Salesforce release. As of recent releases:\n\n- Supported: Account, Contact, Lead, Opportunity, Case (generally available but check the latest release notes for any remaining limitations).\n\n- Not supported: Person Accounts (limited), some Service Cloud objects.\n\nAlways verify in the current release notes, as Salesforce has been rolling out standard object support incrementally. In the exam context, know that custom objects have full support and some standard objects are still gaining it."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj2_q7", number: 61, question: "Service Cloud Console app: the difference between split view and workspace tabs, and how keyboard shortcuts reduce agent handle time in the console", answer: "- Workspace tabs: Each case/record opens as its own tab at the top of the console. Sub-tabs (e.g., related records) appear below the main tab. Agents can work on multiple records without losing context.\n\n- Split view: A collapsible panel on the left side of the console displaying a list view. Agents can click through records in the list without opening new tabs, keeping the list visible alongside the record.\n\nKeyboard shortcuts allow agents to perform common actions (next/previous record, open macros, focus chatter, etc.) without using the mouse, directly reducing average handle time."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj2_q8", number: 62, question: "In-App Guidance: how prompt types (targeted prompts, walkthroughs, floating prompts) are configured to guide users on a Lightning page, and which user license is required to create in-app guidance", answer: "Prompt types:\n\n- Floating prompt: A small popup that appears anywhere on the screen. Not anchored to a specific element.\n\n- Targeted prompt: Anchored to a specific UI element on the page (button, field, component). Highlights the element.\n\n- Walkthrough: A series of sequential prompts guiding users through a multi-step process on the page.\n\nIn-App Guidance is created via Setup > In-App Guidance. Creating and editing prompts requires a user with the \"Manage In-App Guidance\" permission, typically a System Administrator. No special add-on license is required — it is available in Lightning Experience for Enterprise Edition and above."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "objects_apps_obj2_q9", number: 63, question: "Lightning page Analyze button: what the performance analysis surfaces (slow-loading components, components with errors) and when an admin should use it vs. Debug Mode", answer: "The Analyze button in Lightning App Builder scans the page and surfaces:\n\n- Components that are slow to load (identified as performance bottlenecks).\n\n- Components that have errors (configuration issues or missing dependencies).\n\n- Recommendations to improve page performance (e.g., move slow components below the fold).\n\nUse Analyze when users report slow page load times or when you want to proactively audit a complex record page.\n\nDebug Mode (enabled per user in Setup) gives developers full, un-minified JavaScript and detailed console logs for LWC and Aura component debugging. Use Debug Mode for component-level code issues, not for general page performance.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Auditing and Monitoring (3 objectives, 13 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "auditing_monitoring",
            title: "Auditing and Monitoring",
            color: Color(red: 0.55, green: 0.35, blue: 0.75),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.94, green: 0.90, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "eye.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "auditing_monitoring_obj1",
                    title: "Given a scenario, determine the appropriate tools for monitoring and troubleshooting system activity (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj1_q1", number: 64, question: "Setup Audit Trail: what categories of changes it captures vs. what it does NOT track", answer: "Captures: Changes made in Setup — creating/modifying profiles, permission sets, custom fields, page layouts, flows, custom objects, sharing rules, company settings, installed packages, user creation/deactivation, and more.\n\nDoes NOT track:\n\n- Changes to record data (field value changes on business records).\n\n- Running of reports or dashboards.\n\n- Data exports or imports.\n\n- Changes made by the Salesforce system itself (e.g., automated system upgrades).\n\n- Viewing of records (read activity).\n\nSetup Audit Trail is specifically for configuration changes in Setup, not data-level activity."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj1_q2", number: 65, question: "Setup Audit Trail: the retention period for audit entries (last 180 days viewable in UI, up to 6 months downloadable as CSV)", answer: "The Setup Audit Trail shows the last 180 days of changes directly in the UI (up to 20 recent entries on the page). You can download a CSV of the full 6 months of history. Anything older than 6 months is not retained by Salesforce. For longer retention, export the CSV regularly and store it in an external system as part of your compliance strategy."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj1_q3", number: 66, question: "Field History Tracking: the maximum number of tracked fields per object (20) and the UI retention period (18 months)", answer: "- Max tracked fields per object: 20.\n\n- UI retention: The related list on a record shows field history for the last 18 months.\n\n- Field History Tracking records: who changed a field, when, what the old value was, and what the new value is.\n\n- Once you enable tracking on a field, history is only captured going forward, not retroactively."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj1_q4", number: 67, question: "Field History Tracking: how to access data older than 18 months (Data Loader or API query on FieldHistory objects)", answer: "Field history older than 18 months is no longer visible in the UI, but Salesforce retains it for up to 18 months total in the database (accessible via API). To access historical data approaching or at the limit:\n\n- Use Data Loader or a SOQL query via API against the relevant `__History` object (e.g., `AccountHistory`, `OpportunityFieldHistory`).\n\n- For long-term retention beyond 18 months, export field history records regularly to an external data store or use Salesforce Shield Field Audit Trail, which extends retention up to 10 years."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj1_q5", number: 68, question: "Login History: what information is captured, how long it is retained (6 months), and how to query it for compliance investigations", answer: "Captured data: Username, login date/time, source IP address, login type (UI, API, SSO, etc.), browser/platform, login status (Success, Failed, etc.), and the geographic location (when available).\n\nRetention: 6 months.\n\nQuerying: Viewable in Setup > Login History, filterable by user and date. For programmatic access, query the `LoginHistory` object via SOQL or export via Data Loader. For compliance investigations, filter by user, date range, and status to identify unauthorized access attempts or anomalous login patterns."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj1_q6", number: 69, question: "Health Check: what the score represents (percentage of security settings meeting Salesforce Baseline Standard) and what specific security settings it evaluates", answer: "The Health Check score is a percentage (0–100) representing how many of your org's security settings meet or exceed the Salesforce Baseline Standard. A higher score means more settings are configured to Salesforce's recommended security posture.\n\nEvaluated settings include:\n\n- Password policies (length, complexity, expiration, lockout threshold).\n\n- Session security settings (timeout, secure connections, clickjack protection).\n\n- Certificate and network access settings.\n\n- Guest user access settings.\n\n- Login IP ranges and trusted IPs."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj1_q7", number: 70, question: "Health Check: which settings reduce the score and how to improve it", answer: "Settings that most commonly reduce the score:\n\n- Short session timeouts or very long ones (allowing extended idle sessions).\n\n- Weak password policies (short minimum length, no complexity requirements, no expiration).\n\n- No lockout after failed attempts.\n\n- Guest user access that is too permissive.\n\n- HTTP connections allowed (instead of HTTPS-only).\n\nTo improve the score: Click any failing setting in Health Check to navigate directly to the configuration page and apply the recommended value. Salesforce shows the current value, the recommended value, and the risk level (High, Medium, Low).")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "auditing_monitoring_obj2",
                    title: "Explain how to ensure sensitive data is set up to support a business/legal/compliance use case in production and sandbox environments.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj2_q1", number: 71, question: "Shield Platform Encryption vs. Classic Encryption: which fields can be encrypted with each", answer: "| | Classic Encryption | Shield Platform Encryption |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Field types | Custom text fields only (adds a \"masking\" encryption) | Standard and custom fields: text, text area, date, date/time, email, phone, URL, number, and more |\n\n| File encryption | No | Yes (files and attachments) |\n\n| Standard fields | No | Yes |\n\nShield Platform Encryption is the enterprise-grade solution. Classic Encryption is a limited, legacy feature that only masks a custom text field."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj2_q2", number: 72, question: "Shield Platform Encryption vs. Classic Encryption: whether encrypted fields are searchable/filterable and which one encrypts data at rest in the database", answer: "- Classic Encryption: Fields are not searchable via SOQL or standard search. They display as masked values (asterisks) in the UI.\n\n- Shield Platform Encryption: Encrypts data at rest in the database using AES-256. With Deterministic Encryption, fields can be used in exact-match filters and SOQL WHERE clauses. With Probabilistic Encryption (the default), fields are not searchable/filterable.\n\nOnly Shield Platform Encryption truly encrypts data at rest. Classic Encryption is UI masking, not true at-rest encryption."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj2_q3", number: 73, question: "Full Copy sandbox: the only sandbox type that replicates all production data; why it is used for compliance testing and what its refresh interval restriction is (29-day minimum)", answer: "A Full Copy sandbox is an exact replica of production — all metadata AND all data records. It is used for:\n\n- Compliance testing: Testing with real (or real-volume) production data.\n\n- Performance testing: Load testing with production data volumes.\n\n- Disaster recovery drills.\n\nRefresh interval: Minimum 29 days between refreshes. This is the longest refresh restriction of all sandbox types, reflecting the cost and time of copying an entire production org. Because it holds real data, access must be carefully controlled to meet compliance requirements."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj2_q4", number: 74, question: "Sandbox data masking: how to prevent sensitive production data (SSNs, credit card numbers) from appearing in non-full sandboxes, including the use of data mask tools and sandbox templates", answer: "For Partial Copy sandboxes, use sandbox templates to control which objects are included in the copy and limit the volume of sensitive data. Beyond templates, use Data Mask (a Salesforce-native tool) or third-party data anonymization tools to scramble or replace sensitive field values (SSNs, credit card numbers, email addresses) after refresh. For Developer and Developer Pro sandboxes, no production data is included by default. Establish a process where any sandbox refresh that includes production data triggers an automated masking job before granting developer access.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "auditing_monitoring_obj3",
                    title: "Explain how to review and troubleshoot security settings including pending updates that may change system access.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj3_q1", number: 75, question: "Critical Updates: how to review and activate/deactivate pending critical updates before their auto-activation date, and the risk of not testing them in a sandbox first", answer: "Critical Updates (now called Release Updates in newer Salesforce versions) are changes Salesforce plans to auto-activate in a future release to improve security, performance, or compliance. Admins can review them in Setup > Release Updates.\n\n- Activate early: Test in a sandbox, then activate in production before the auto-activation date to control timing.\n\n- Deactivate temporarily: Some updates can be deactivated to buy more time, but they will auto-activate eventually.\n\n- Risk of not testing: Auto-activation in production without sandbox testing can break flows, integrations, page layouts, or sharing behavior. Always test in a sandbox first to identify impact."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "auditing_monitoring_obj3_q2", number: 76, question: "Security Health Check high-risk settings: identifying which specific configuration changes (e.g., session timeout, password complexity, IP restrictions) most significantly improve the Health Check score", answer: "High-risk settings (those that most impact the score when misconfigured):\n\n- Session timeout: Setting it too long (e.g., 8 hours) is flagged as high-risk. Recommended: 2 hours or less.\n\n- Password minimum length: Shorter than 8 characters is high-risk. Recommended: 8+ characters.\n\n- Password complexity: Not requiring uppercase, numbers, or special characters is high-risk.\n\n- Maximum invalid login attempts: Setting it too high (or no lockout) is high-risk. Recommended: 5 or fewer attempts.\n\n- Require secure connections (HTTPS): Not requiring HTTPS is high-risk.\n\n- Guest user access: Granting excessive object permissions to guest users is flagged.\n\nFixing high-risk items yields the greatest score improvement.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Cloud Applications (6 objectives, 30 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "cloud_apps",
            title: "Cloud Applications",
            color: Color(red: 0.80, green: 0.40, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 1.00, green: 0.94, blue: 0.88),
            icon: "cloud.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cloud_apps_obj1",
                    title: "Describe the features of Salesforce which enable Sales users to conduct the sale process, including products, price books, schedules, orders, and quotes.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj1_q1", number: 77, question: "Products and Price Books: how a product must be added to at least one price book before it can be added to an opportunity, and the role of the Standard Price Book vs. custom price books", answer: "Every product must have an entry in at least one price book (as a Price Book Entry) before it can be added to an opportunity. The Standard Price Book is the system-default price book. Every org has exactly one. A product's entry in the Standard Price Book is its \"list price.\" Custom price books allow different pricing for different markets, regions, or customer segments. A product must first be added to the Standard Price Book, and then it can be added to a custom price book with a different price. When adding products to an opportunity, the user selects a price book first, then chooses products from that book."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj1_q2", number: 78, question: "Product Schedules: the difference between quantity schedules, revenue schedules, and combined schedules, and which object stores the schedule data", answer: "- Quantity schedules: Define when and how much of a product is delivered (e.g., 10 units in January, 10 in February). Used for physical goods.\n\n- Revenue schedules: Define when revenue is recognized (e.g., $500/month over 12 months). Used for services or subscriptions.\n\n- Combined schedules: Track both quantity delivery and revenue recognition independently on the same product.\n\nSchedule data is stored on the Opportunity Product (OpportunityLineItem) object and its child schedule records (`OpportunityLineItemSchedule`). Schedules do not affect the Opportunity Amount directly unless the schedule amounts are configured to do so."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj1_q3", number: 79, question: "Quotes: the sync behavior between a quote and its parent opportunity (only one quote can be synced at a time; syncing a new quote unsyncs the previous), and what fields sync between the objects", answer: "Only one quote can be synced to an opportunity at a time. When you sync a quote, changes to the quote's line items and totals sync back to the opportunity's products and amounts (and vice versa). Syncing a second quote automatically unsyncs the first. Fields that sync include: opportunity Amount, Close Date, and products/line items. To prevent the quote from being edited independently while synced, the opportunity and quote stay in lockstep. To stop syncing, click \"Unsynce\" on the quote."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj1_q4", number: 80, question: "Orders: how orders are created from contracts or opportunities, the status lifecycle (Draft → Activated), and restrictions on editing an activated order", answer: "Orders can be created from Contracts or directly from Opportunities (depending on configuration). Status lifecycle: Draft → Activated.\n\n- In Draft state: order products can be added, edited, or removed.\n\n- Once Activated: the order is locked. Products cannot be added or removed, and most fields cannot be edited. To make changes, you would need to create a Reduction Order.\n\nOrders require the product to be in a price book matching the account's currency. Activation signals that the order is committed and ready for fulfillment."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj1_q5", number: 81, question: "Account Teams: default team templates, the access level granted to team members, and whether Account Team access overrides OWD restrictions on related opportunities and cases", answer: "Account Teams allow multiple users to collaborate on an account. Each member is assigned a Team Role and an access level (Read Only or Read/Write) to the Account, and optionally to related Contacts, Opportunities, and Cases.\n\nDefault team templates can be created so that users' personal default teams are automatically added to new accounts they own.\n\nOWD interaction: Account Team membership does grant access to related Opportunities and Cases, even if OWD for those objects is Private. The access level is whatever is configured on the team membership, not exceeding Read/Write. This is one of the few declarative ways to grant access to related records beyond OWD without sharing rules."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj1_q6", number: 82, question: "Opportunity Teams: how they differ from Account Teams in terms of member roles and split percentages, and how Opportunity Team sharing interacts with the base OWD", answer: "Opportunity Teams are specific to a single opportunity. Each team member has a Role (e.g., Account Executive, Sales Engineer) and an access level. Opportunity Teams also support split percentages for tracking each member's contribution to a deal (used in overlay or split forecasting). Account Teams are account-wide; Opportunity Teams are deal-specific. Opportunity Team membership grants access to that specific opportunity regardless of OWD, similar to how Account Teams work. The access granted is the level set on the membership record.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cloud_apps_obj2",
                    title: "Distinguish and understand the implications and capabilities of Forecasting and Territory Management.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj2_q1", number: 83, question: "Collaborative Forecasting: the forecast hierarchy vs. the role hierarchy, how forecast adjustments work (manager can adjust subordinate's forecast without changing the subordinate's own number), and the effect of including opportunity from a forecast", answer: "Forecast hierarchy: Separate from the role hierarchy. It must be explicitly configured and maps to managers and their direct reports for forecasting purposes.\n\nForecast adjustments: A manager can adjust a subordinate's forecast number upward or downward. This adjustment is additive to the rollup — the subordinate's own forecast number is unchanged. The manager's view shows their adjustment separately from the subordinate's submitted amount.\n\nInclude/Exclude from forecast: Setting an opportunity's Forecast Category to \"Omitted\" removes it from the forecast rollup entirely. Moving it back to any other category re-includes it."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj2_q2", number: 84, question: "Forecast categories: the mapping from Opportunity Stage to forecast category (Pipeline, Best Case, Commit, Closed, Omitted) and how a custom stage maps to a forecast category", answer: "Standard mapping:\n\n| Forecast Category | Typical Stage Mapping |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| Pipeline | Early stages (e.g., Prospecting, Qualification) |\n\n| Best Case | Mid-probability stages (e.g., Proposal) |\n\n| Commit | High-probability stages (e.g., Negotiation) |\n\n| Closed | Closed Won |\n\n| Omitted | Closed Lost, or explicitly excluded |\n\nWhen creating a custom opportunity stage, the admin must manually map it to one of these forecast categories. The mapping is done in the Stage picklist configuration. The forecast category determines how the opportunity appears in the forecast rollup."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj2_q3", number: 85, question: "Forecasting types (opportunity revenue, opportunity quantity, product family, overlay): how each type differs and when product family forecasting is enabled", answer: "- Opportunity Revenue: Forecast based on the opportunity's Amount field. Most common type.\n\n- Opportunity Quantity: Forecast based on the total quantity of products on the opportunity.\n\n- Product Family: Forecast rolls up by product family, allowing separate forecast amounts for each family (e.g., Hardware vs. Software). Requires product families to be defined and products categorized.\n\n- Overlay: Tracks overlay/split revenue where multiple reps share credit for a deal. Uses split percentages from Opportunity Teams.\n\nMultiple forecast types can be active simultaneously. Product family forecasting requires enabling product schedules or product families in the org settings."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj2_q4", number: 86, question: "Forecast rollup behavior: how forecasts roll up from the bottom of the forecast hierarchy to the top, what 'cumulative forecast rollup' means vs. standard rollup, and whether closed/won amounts are always included", answer: "Forecasts roll from individual reps up through managers in the forecast hierarchy. Each manager's total includes their own pipeline plus their direct and indirect reports' amounts.\n\n- Standard rollup: Each category (Pipeline, Best Case, Commit, Closed) is shown separately and does not include higher-probability categories.\n\n- Cumulative forecast rollup: Higher categories include the amounts from lower (more likely) categories. For example, \"Best Case\" includes Commit and Closed amounts in addition to Best Case-only deals.\n\nClosed Won amounts are always included in every forecast category rollup (except Omitted) because closed deals are certain revenue."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj2_q5", number: 87, question: "Territory Management 2.0: territory types, territory models, territory hierarchy, and how territory assignment rules fire when an account record is saved", answer: "- Territory Types: Organizational categories for territories (e.g., \"Geographic,\" \"Named Account\"). Used to classify territories, not for access control.\n\n- Territory Models: The container for an entire territory structure. Only one model can be Active at a time.\n\n- Territory Hierarchy: The tree of territories within a model (parent/child relationships, e.g., North America > West > California).\n\n- Assignment rules: Each territory has filter criteria. When an account is saved, Salesforce evaluates all active assignment rules in the active model and assigns the account to every territory whose rules it matches. Rules can be run manually for all accounts or fire automatically on account save (if configured)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj2_q6", number: 88, question: "Territory model states (Planning, Active, Archived): which actions (running assignment rules, associating territories to users) require the model to be in Active state", answer: "- Planning: Design mode. You can build the hierarchy, create territories, configure rules, and preview assignments. You cannot run rules org-wide or affect record access.\n\n- Active: The model is live and controls record access. Running assignment rules on all accounts requires the model to be Active. Users are associated to territories only in the Active model (or in Planning for preview/testing purposes, but it has no access effect).\n\n- Archived: Read-only. No changes or rule execution. Used for historical auditing.\n\nOnly one model can be Active at any time. Activating a new model automatically archives the previous one."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj2_q7", number: 89, question: "Territory Management 2.0 and opportunities: how an opportunity appears in a territory forecast and the difference between opportunity territory assignment (manual vs. filter-based)", answer: "An opportunity appears in a territory's forecast when it is assigned to that territory. Assignment can happen two ways:\n\n- Filter-based (automatic): A territory assignment rule on the Opportunity object (separate from Account rules) automatically assigns the opportunity based on field criteria (e.g., close date, revenue range). Requires enabling opportunity filters in territory settings.\n\n- Manual: A user (or admin) directly assigns a territory to the opportunity from the opportunity record.\n\nThe assigned territory determines which territory's forecast the opportunity rolls into. If no territory is assigned, the opportunity does not appear in territory-based forecasting.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cloud_apps_obj3",
                    title: "Explain how to create and maintain Salesforce Knowledge (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj3_q1", number: 90, question: "Knowledge article record types: how they control the article's page layout, fields, and which Lightning record page is displayed to agents and customers", answer: "Knowledge article record types work similarly to record types on other objects. Each record type:\n\n- Controls which fields appear on the article (via page layout assignment).\n\n- Controls which page layout is shown to users.\n\n- In Lightning, determines which Lightning record page is assigned for that article type.\n\n- Can control which channels the article is visible in (internal app, customer portal, partner portal, public knowledge base).\n\nCommon practice is to create separate record types for different article categories (e.g., FAQs, How-To Guides, Known Issues), each with its own layout and channel settings."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj3_q2", number: 91, question: "Knowledge article lifecycle: Draft → Review → Published → Archived, and who has permission to publish vs. archive an article based on the Knowledge User profile permission", answer: "| State | Description |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| Draft | Article is being written/edited. Not visible to end users. |\n\n| Review | Article is pending approval/review. |\n\n| Published | Visible to users in configured channels. |\n\n| Archived | Removed from active channels. Not deleted, but no longer visible. |\n\n- Publishing: Requires the \"Publish Articles\" permission (Knowledge User with publish permission or a profile/permission set granting it).\n\n- Archiving: Requires the \"Archive Articles\" permission. Admins and designated content managers typically have this.\n\nStandard users cannot publish or archive unless explicitly granted the permission."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj3_q3", number: 92, question: "Knowledge article versioning: when a published article is edited, a draft version is created while the published version remains live; the relationship between article versions and how publishing the new draft replaces the previous version", answer: "When a published article is edited, Salesforce creates a new draft version while the current published version remains live and visible to users. The two versions coexist until the draft is published. When the new draft is published, it replaces the previous published version, which is then moved to an archived state (as a previous version, not deleted). Salesforce maintains a version history so previous versions can be referenced. This ensures users always see the current published content while editing is in progress."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj3_q4", number: 93, question: "Data categories in Knowledge: how category groups and categories control article visibility for internal users, partner users, and customer portal users", answer: "Data Category Groups are top-level containers (e.g., \"Products,\" \"Geography\"). Within each group are categories in a hierarchy (e.g., Products > Hardware > Laptops).\n\nArticles are tagged with categories. Visibility is controlled by assigning data category visibility to user profiles:\n\n- Internal users: Can be granted visibility to all or specific categories.\n\n- Partner users: Visibility configured per portal profile.\n\n- Customer users: Visibility configured per community/portal profile.\n\nUsers only see articles tagged with categories they have visibility to. This allows fine-grained content access control without duplicating articles.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cloud_apps_obj4",
                    title: "Explain how to create and maintain service entitlements and entitlement processes.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj4_q1", number: 94, question: "Entitlements vs. entitlement processes: an entitlement defines what support a customer is eligible for; an entitlement process defines the SLA timeline with milestones and automated actions", answer: "- Entitlement: A record that defines what support a customer is entitled to — for example, \"Phone Support,\" \"24/7 Support,\" or \"Email Only.\" Entitlements link to Accounts, Contacts, or Assets. They answer: does this customer have support, and what kind?\n\n- Entitlement Process: A timeline of SLA milestones attached to an entitlement. It defines how long an agent has to respond, resolve, or escalate a case — and what automated actions fire (emails, field updates) when milestones are hit or missed.\n\nThink of entitlements as the contract and the entitlement process as the SLA clock."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj4_q2", number: 95, question: "Entitlement lookup on a case: how the entitlement lookup on a case is auto-populated based on Account or Contact matching and what the 'Set Default Entitlement' entitlement setting does", answer: "When a case is created or an Account/Contact is set on a case, Salesforce can auto-populate the Entitlement field by matching the Account or Contact to an active entitlement for that customer. The \"Set Default Entitlement\" option on an entitlement record marks it as the one to auto-apply when a matching case is created for that account or contact. If multiple entitlements exist for a customer, the one marked as default is chosen automatically. Without this setting, agents must manually select the entitlement."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj4_q3", number: 96, question: "Entitlement milestones: how milestone actions (warning and violation) fire before and after the milestone completion time, and what happens to milestone timers when a case is closed", answer: "Milestones are required steps in an entitlement process (e.g., \"First Response within 2 hours,\" \"Resolution within 8 hours\"). Each milestone has:\n\n- Warning actions: Fire a set time before the milestone deadline (e.g., email the agent 30 minutes before the 2-hour mark).\n\n- Violation actions: Fire if the milestone is not completed by the deadline (e.g., escalate the case, notify a manager).\n\nWhen a case is closed, all milestone timers stop. If a case is reopened, timers may resume depending on configuration. Completed milestones are not affected by case closure."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj4_q4", number: 97, question: "Entitlement processes and business hours: how associating business hours with an entitlement process affects milestone completion time calculations (only business hours count)", answer: "When business hours are associated with an entitlement process, milestone timers only count time during configured business hours. For example, if business hours are 9 AM to 5 PM Monday-Friday, and a case is opened at 4 PM Friday, a 4-hour response milestone deadline would be 10 AM Monday (not 8 PM Friday). This ensures SLA calculations reflect actual working time. Entitlement processes without business hours count 24/7 clock time. Each entitlement process can reference a specific set of business hours.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cloud_apps_obj5",
                    title: "Describe the features of Salesforce which enable interaction between support agents and customers (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj5_q1", number: 98, question: "Case Feed: which publisher actions are available by default (Log a Call, Send Email, Change Status) and how to add custom quick actions to the Case Feed publisher", answer: "Default publisher actions in Case Feed: Log a Call, Send Email, Change Status.\n\nTo add custom actions: Create a quick action on the Case object (object-specific action), then add it to the Case Feed's publisher layout (Case Feed Layout or Global Publisher Layout, depending on org setup). In Lightning, actions added to the Case page layout's \"Highlights Panel\" and \"Quick Actions in the Salesforce Classic Publisher\" section appear in the Case Feed action bar. Custom quick actions can trigger flows, create records, or update fields."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj5_q2", number: 99, question: "Web-to-Case: how HTML forms submit customer inquiries directly as cases, the daily case creation limit (5,000 per day), and how assignment rules interact with Web-to-Case submissions", answer: "Web-to-Case generates an HTML form that customers submit on a website. Salesforce converts submissions into Case records automatically.\n\n- Daily limit: 5,000 cases per day via Web-to-Case. Submissions beyond the limit are sent to the Default Case Owner.\n\n- Assignment rules: Standard Case Assignment Rules fire on Web-to-Case submissions, just as they do on manually created cases. Configure an active assignment rule to route web cases to the appropriate queue or user.\n\n- The default Case Status and Case Origin (Web) are set automatically."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj5_q3", number: 100, question: "Email-to-Case vs. On-Demand Email-to-Case: the difference in how emails are routed (on-demand uses Salesforce-generated email address; standard requires firewall routing), and the email-to-case attachment size limits", answer: "| | Email-to-Case | On-Demand Email-to-Case |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Routing | Emails sent to your email server, forwarded to Salesforce via a local agent behind your firewall | Emails sent directly to a Salesforce-generated address (no firewall agent needed) |\n\n| Setup | Requires installing a local agent on a server | Simpler — just configure the Salesforce email address |\n\n| Attachment limit | 25 MB per email | 25 MB per email |\n\nOn-Demand is easier to set up. Standard Email-to-Case is used when emails must pass through your own mail server for compliance or filtering."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj5_q4", number: 101, question: "Omni-Channel routing models: queue-based routing (pushes work from queue to available agents by priority/capacity) vs. skill-based routing (routes to agent with matching skills) vs. external routing", answer: "- Queue-Based Routing: Work items (cases, chats, leads) are pushed from a queue to the most available agent based on the queue's priority and the agent's capacity. Agents must be members of the queue.\n\n- Skill-Based Routing: Routes work to agents who have the specific skills required by the work item (e.g., \"Spanish Language,\" \"Billing Expert\"). Skills are assigned to agents and required on routing configurations.\n\n- External Routing: Work is routed by a third-party system (e.g., a custom telephony platform) via the Omni-Channel APIs. Salesforce provides the presence framework; routing logic lives externally."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj5_q5", number: 102, question: "Omni-Channel presence statuses: how presence configurations control which channels an agent can work on simultaneously and what capacity limits apply", answer: "Presence Statuses (e.g., \"Available - Phone,\" \"Available - Chat,\" \"Busy\") are created by admins and assigned to Service Channels via a Presence Configuration. The Presence Configuration defines:\n\n- Which statuses are available to agents.\n\n- The agent's capacity — the maximum number of work items they can handle simultaneously (measured in capacity units, where each channel's routing config assigns a capacity cost per work item).\n\nAgents select their status in the Omni-Channel widget. Choosing \"Available - Chat\" makes them eligible only for chat work items. Capacity limits prevent agents from being overloaded."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj5_q6", number: 103, question: "Omni-Channel service channels: how service channels map work items (Cases, Chats, Social Posts, Leads) to Omni-Channel routing, and what happens when a work item reaches a queue that has no available agents", answer: "Service Channels map Salesforce objects to Omni-Channel routing. You create a service channel for Cases, one for Chats, one for Leads, etc., and assign a capacity cost per work item. When a new work item (e.g., a new Case) is created and meets routing criteria (via a Flow or Omni-Channel routing configuration), it is placed into the configured queue.\n\nIf no agents are available in that queue (all at capacity or offline), the work item stays in the queue until an agent becomes available. Work is not lost — it simply waits. Admins can configure queue prioritization to ensure high-priority items are routed first when capacity opens up."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj5_q7", number: 104, question: "Experience Cloud community access levels: Guest User vs. Authenticated Customer/Partner User, and which license type (Customer Community, Customer Community Plus, Partner Community) grants which Salesforce object access", answer: "| License | Object Access |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| Guest User (no login) | Public-facing pages only. Very limited, read-only access to objects explicitly shared. No standard object editing. |\n\n| Customer Community | Accounts, Contacts, Cases, and Knowledge. Cannot access standard reporting or share records with other community members (limited visibility). |\n\n| Customer Community Plus | Same as Customer Community plus: full sharing rules support, reports, dashboards, and more object access. |\n\n| Partner Community | Full CRM object access relevant to partners: Leads, Opportunities, Campaigns, Contacts, Accounts, Cases, plus Salesforce Reports and Dashboards. Most powerful of the three. |")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cloud_apps_obj6",
                    title: "Given a scenario, understand the standard Salesforce suite of products that enable extending the core platform.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj6_q1", number: 105, question: "AppExchange managed vs. unmanaged packages: what differentiates them in terms of upgradeability, code visibility, and namespace locking", answer: "| | Managed Package | Unmanaged Package |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Upgradeability | Publisher can push upgrades to subscribers | No upgrade path; each install is a snapshot |\n\n| Code visibility | Apex classes/triggers are protected (cannot be viewed) | All metadata is fully visible and editable |\n\n| Namespace | Has a locked namespace prefix (e.g., `mypkg__Field__c`) | No namespace; components go into the default org namespace |\n\n| Deletion by subscriber | Cannot delete managed components | Can delete any installed component |\n\nManaged packages are for ISV products. Unmanaged packages are for open templates or internal distribution."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cloud_apps_obj6_q2", number: 106, question: "Salesforce Connect and External Objects: how external objects use OData adapters to surface data from external systems without storing it in Salesforce, and the query limitations compared to standard objects", answer: "Salesforce Connect uses an OData 2.0 or 4.0 adapter (or custom adapter) to create External Objects that map to data in an external database or API. Data is not stored in Salesforce — queries are passed through to the external system in real time. This is useful for surfacing large datasets (ERP data, legacy systems) without ETL.\n\nLimitations vs. standard objects:\n\n- Cannot be used in standard Salesforce reports.\n\n- Cannot have roll-up summary fields from external objects to standard objects.\n\n- SOQL on external objects is passed through to the external source; all governor limits still apply.\n\n- No triggers, flows, or workflow rules on external objects.\n\n- Query performance depends on the external system's response time.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Data and Analytics Management (4 objectives, 29 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "data_analytics",
            title: "Data and Analytics Management",
            color: Color(red: 0.25, green: 0.55, blue: 0.70),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.95, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "chart.bar.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "data_analytics_obj1",
                    title: "Explain the tools and best practices for assessing, improving, and enriching data quality (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj1_q1", number: 107, question: "Custom field data types: the difference between Text, Text Area, Long Text Area, and Rich Text Area in terms of character limits", answer: "| Type | Character Limit | Notes |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Text | Up to 255 | Single line. Searchable, filterable. |\n\n| Text Area | Up to 255 | Multi-line (but limited). Searchable. |\n\n| Long Text Area | Up to 131,072 | Multi-line. Not indexed/searchable via standard search. |\n\n| Rich Text Area | Up to 131,072 | Multi-line with HTML formatting. Not indexed. |\n\nFor search and filtering, use Text or Text Area. For large freeform content, use Long Text Area. For formatted content (bold, bullets, images), use Rich Text Area."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj1_q2", number: 108, question: "Custom field data types: which field types can be used in reports, SOQL WHERE clauses, or duplicate matching rules", answer: "- Reports: Most field types are available as report columns and filters, including Text, Number, Date, Picklist, Checkbox, Currency, Email, Phone, URL. Long Text Area and Rich Text Area are not filterable in reports.\n\n- SOQL WHERE clauses: Text, Number, Date, Picklist, Checkbox, Currency, Email, Phone can all be filtered. Long Text Area and Rich Text Area cannot be used in WHERE clauses.\n\n- Duplicate Matching Rules: Support Text, Email, Phone, Name fields. Long Text Area, Rich Text Area, and most complex types are not supported in matching rules."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj1_q3", number: 109, question: "Validation rules: order of evaluation relative to required field checks and other automation, and what happens when a before-save Flow and a validation rule both fire on the same record save", answer: "Order of execution on save (simplified):"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj1_q4", number: 110, question: "System checks (required fields at the platform level).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj1_q5", number: 111, question: "**Before-save Flows** run (can modify field values).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj1_q6", number: 112, question: "**Validation rules** evaluate — they see the field values as modified by the before-save flow.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj1_q7", number: 113, question: "If validation passes: record is committed (DML).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj1_q8", number: 114, question: "After-save Flows and other automation run.", answer: "If a before-save flow changes a field value, the validation rule evaluates the new value (post-flow). This means a before-save flow can be used to set values that help a record pass validation — or inadvertently cause a record to fail validation if the flow sets a bad value."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj1_q9", number: 115, question: "Duplicate Management: how Matching Rules and Duplicate Rules work together, the difference between Alert and Block actions, and when a bypass permission overrides a Block", answer: "- Matching Rules: Define the algorithm for detecting duplicates (which fields to compare, how to compare them — exact, fuzzy, etc.). Salesforce provides standard matching rules for Contacts, Leads, and Accounts.\n\n- Duplicate Rules: Reference a matching rule and define the action to take when a duplicate is detected: Alert (warn but allow save) or Block (prevent save).\n\nAlert: User sees a warning and can still save if they choose. Best for scenarios where some duplicates are acceptable.\n\nBlock: User cannot save the record until the potential duplicate issue is resolved.\n\nBypass permission: Users with the \"Bypass Duplicate Prevention\" permission can save a record even when a Block action fires. Typically granted only to data stewards or admins."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj1_q10", number: 116, question: "Duplicate rules scope: how an org-wide duplicate rule can be configured to match across different objects (e.g., Leads against Contacts) using a cross-object matching rule, and what happens when a matching record is found on a different object", answer: "When creating a Duplicate Rule, you can reference a cross-object matching rule that compares a Lead being saved, for example, against existing Contacts. If a matching Contact is found, the Duplicate Rule fires its Alert or Block action. The user sees the matching Contact records as potential duplicates, even though they are a different object type. This prevents creating Leads for people who are already Contacts in the system. The action (alert/block) and the matching object are all configured on the Duplicate Rule.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "data_analytics_obj2",
                    title: "Given a scenario, determine the appropriate analytic tools to meet complex reporting requirements (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q1", number: 117, question: "Report formats (Tabular, Summary, Matrix, Joined): which format supports grouping, subtotals, cross-object comparison, and which is required for reporting snapshots or dashboard components", answer: "| Format | Grouping | Subtotals | Cross-Object | Dashboard | Snapshot |\n\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n| Tabular | No | No | No | No (list only) | No |\n\n| Summary | Yes (rows) | Yes | No | Yes | Yes |\n\n| Matrix | Yes (rows + cols) | Yes | No | Yes | Yes |\n\n| Joined | Yes (per block) | Yes | Yes (multiple blocks) | No | No |\n\nTabular reports are flat lists. Summary and Matrix are required for dashboards and snapshots. Joined reports allow comparing data from different report types side by side."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q2", number: 118, question: "Joined reports: maximum number of blocks (5), ability to use different primary objects per block, requirement that all blocks share at least one common field, and what chart type is available for joined reports", answer: "- Maximum blocks: 5 per joined report.\n\n- Different primary objects: Each block can have a different primary object (e.g., Block 1 = Accounts, Block 2 = Opportunities).\n\n- Common field requirement: All blocks must share at least one common field (typically a standard field like Account Name or Report Date) to allow cross-block analysis.\n\n- Available chart types: Joined reports support only the vertical bar chart (grouped bar chart). They do not support all chart types available to summary/matrix reports."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q3", number: 119, question: "Custom Report Types: when a standard report type is insufficient (need fields from objects without a pre-built report type, need up to 4 related objects in a relationship chain) and the primary vs. related object configuration", answer: "Use a Custom Report Type (CRT) when:\n\n- You need fields from objects that do not have a standard report type.\n\n- You need to traverse a relationship chain up to 4 objects deep (e.g., Account → Opportunity → Opportunity Product → Product).\n\n- You need to add fields from related objects not available in standard types.\n\nIn a CRT, you define the primary object (the anchor, e.g., Account) and then optionally add up to 3 related objects. The \"with\" vs. \"with or without\" relationship setting determines whether parent records without children are included."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q4", number: 120, question: "Custom Report Type relationship rows: the 'with' vs. 'with or without' relationship setting between objects in a CRT determines whether parent records without any children are included in the report results", answer: "- \"A must have B\" (with): Only parent records that have at least one related child record of the specified type are included. Parent records without any children are excluded.\n\n- \"A may or may not have B\" (with or without): All parent records are included, whether or not they have related children. Rows without children will have blank values for child fields.\n\nThis setting is critical when you want to find records that have no related children (use \"with or without\" and filter for blank child fields)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q5", number: 121, question: "Bucket fields in reports: how bucketing groups numeric, picklist, or text field values without creating a custom formula field, and the limitation that bucket fields cannot be used in cross-report formulas", answer: "A Bucket field lets you group field values into named categories directly in a report without creating a custom field. For example, group Annual Revenue into \"Small (<$100K),\" \"Medium ($100K-$1M),\" \"Large (>$1M).\"\n\nSupported field types: Number/Currency, Picklist, Text.\n\nLimitation: Bucket fields created within a report are local to that report and cannot be referenced in cross-report formulas or used in other reports. They are also not available in dashboards as standalone filters."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q6", number: 122, question: "Cross filters: how 'With' and 'Without' subfilters work to include/exclude records based on the presence or absence of related child records, and a scenario where cross filters are the only declarative solution", answer: "Cross filters add a subfilter to a report based on related objects without needing to create a CRT.\n\n- \"With [related object]\": Include only parent records that have at least one related child record meeting specified criteria.\n\n- \"Without [related object]\": Include only parent records that have NO related child records meeting specified criteria.\n\nExample scenario: \"Show all Accounts that have NO open Opportunities.\" This requires a cross filter (Account Without Opportunities where Stage != Closed). There is no other declarative report approach that achieves this without cross filters."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q7", number: 123, question: "Custom Summary Formulas: the PARENTGROUPVAL function (references a value from a higher grouping level) vs. PREVGROUPVAL (references the value from the previous peer group at the same level), and where they can be placed in a summary report", answer: "- PARENTGROUPVAL: References a summary value at a higher (parent) grouping level. Use case: calculate each row's value as a percentage of its group total (e.g., each rep's revenue / total team revenue).\n\n- PREVGROUPVAL: References the summary value from the previous peer group at the same level. Use case: calculate month-over-month change (this month's revenue minus last month's revenue).\n\nCustom Summary Formulas can only be placed in the summary rows of a Summary or Matrix report — at grouping levels, not at the record (detail) row level."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q8", number: 124, question: "Dynamic dashboards: data is run as the logged-in viewer, the license limit on the number of dynamic dashboards per org, and scenarios where a dynamic dashboard is required over a standard dashboard", answer: "A Dynamic Dashboard runs its component reports as the logged-in user who views the dashboard, rather than as a fixed running user. Each viewer sees data they have access to, enforcing record-level security.\n\nLicense limits: The number of dynamic dashboards depends on edition (Enterprise: up to 5; Unlimited: up to 10; Performance: up to 10 — check current limits as Salesforce updates them).\n\nWhen required: Any time you need a single dashboard that shows each user their own data (e.g., each sales rep sees only their own pipeline), without building a separate dashboard per user."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q9", number: 125, question: "Dashboard filters: how adding a filter to a dashboard affects all components simultaneously, the maximum number of dashboard filters allowed per dashboard (3), and which component types do not support filters", answer: "Dashboard filters allow viewers to interactively slice dashboard data without editing the dashboard. Adding a filter applies it to all components simultaneously (where supported).\n\n- Maximum filters per dashboard: 3.\n\n- Component types that do NOT support filters: Visualforce components, S-controls, and any chart type that is not fed by a report. Standard report-based components (charts, tables, metrics, gauges) generally support filters.\n\nFilters do not create new reports — they apply WHERE clause conditions to the existing reports feeding the dashboard."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q10", number: 126, question: "Reporting Snapshots: how a scheduled report's summarized data is stored in a custom object for historical trending, including which report format is required (summary or matrix) and how to map report columns to custom object fields", answer: "A Reporting Snapshot schedules a report to run automatically and stores the results in a custom object, creating a historical record at that point in time.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- The source report must be Summary or Matrix format (not tabular or joined).\n\n- A custom object must exist with fields that map to the report's summary columns.\n\n- The admin maps each report column to a custom object field in the Reporting Snapshot configuration.\n\n- A schedule is set (daily, weekly, etc.).\n\nEach scheduled run creates new records in the custom object, enabling historical trend analysis over time."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj2_q11", number: 127, question: "Historical trending reports: how they capture point-in-time snapshots of up to 8 fields on supported objects (Opportunities, Cases, Leads, Forecasting items) and the limitation that historical data is available for only the past 3 months", answer: "Historical Trending allows you to report on how field values changed over time, using Salesforce's built-in historical data capture (separate from reporting snapshots).\n\n- Supported objects: Opportunities, Cases, Leads, Forecasting Items.\n\n- Max tracked fields: 8 per object (selected by admin).\n\n- Data retention: Historical data is available for only the past 3 months. Data older than 3 months is not accessible in historical trend reports.\n\n- Reports show field values as they were on specific dates, enabling trend analysis like \"how did Opportunity Amount change over the past 90 days.\"")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "data_analytics_obj3",
                    title: "Understand methods to connect to, access, backup/restore, or archive data outside of the Salesforce Platform (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj3_q1", number: 128, question: "Data backup options: Salesforce Weekly Export vs. Data Loader for scheduled exports, and how to use a third-party backup solution to meet an RPO of less than 24 hours", answer: "- Salesforce Weekly Export: Built-in tool in Setup that exports data as CSV files. Available weekly (or daily in some editions). Simple but infrequent — does not meet tight RPO requirements.\n\n- Data Loader: Can be scheduled via the command line/batch scripts to run on any frequency (e.g., nightly). More flexible than Weekly Export.\n\n- Third-party backup solutions (e.g., OwnBackup, Spanning): Provide continuous or near-real-time backup with RPO of less than 24 hours, point-in-time restore, and metadata backup. Required when business or legal requirements demand sub-24-hour RPO.\n\nNote: Salesforce itself does not guarantee data restoration for customer-deleted records; having a third-party backup is a best practice."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj3_q2", number: 129, question: "Big Objects: how they differ from standard/custom objects (no triggers, no flows, limited SOQL support), the Index fields requirement for querying, and their primary use case for data archiving", answer: "| Feature | Standard/Custom Objects | Big Objects |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Triggers | Yes | No |\n\n| Flows/Workflow | Yes | No |\n\n| SOQL filtering | Any indexed field | Only defined Index fields |\n\n| Storage capacity | Governed by org limits | Massive (billions of records) |\n\n| Reports/Dashboards | Yes | No |\n\nBig Objects are designed for data archiving — storing massive volumes of historical data (e.g., audit logs, event data, old records) that must be retained but not actively queried in standard reports. Querying requires using the exact Index fields in the SOQL WHERE clause in the correct index order."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj3_q3", number: 130, question: "Salesforce Connect and External Objects: how OData 2.0/4.0 adapters expose external data without ETL, the governor limits that apply when querying external objects, and the limitation that external objects cannot be used in standard reports", answer: "Salesforce Connect uses OData adapters to create External Objects that proxy queries to an external data source in real time. No data is copied into Salesforce (no ETL required).\n\nGovernor limits on external objects:\n\n- Each query to an external object counts against the Salesforce Connect external callout limits (200 per transaction in some contexts).\n\n- Standard Apex governor limits for callouts apply.\n\n- Response time is dependent on the external system.\n\nLimitation: External Objects cannot be used in standard Salesforce reports or dashboards. To report on external data, you must bring it into Salesforce via ETL or use a third-party reporting tool that connects to the external source directly.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "data_analytics_obj4",
                    title: "Given a scenario, identify the appropriate tools and methods for importing data into Salesforce (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj4_q1", number: 131, question: "Data Import Wizard vs. Data Loader: which objects each supports, which tool supports upsert operations, and the maximum record count for Import Wizard vs. Data Loader", answer: "| Feature | Data Import Wizard | Data Loader |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Objects supported | Standard objects: Accounts, Contacts, Leads, Solutions, Campaign Members, and custom objects | Any standard or custom object |\n\n| Upsert | Yes | Yes |\n\n| Insert, Update, Delete | Insert and Update | Insert, Update, Upsert, Delete, Export, Export All |\n\n| Max records per import | 50,000 | No practical limit (can handle millions with batch processing) |\n\n| Interface | Browser-based wizard | Desktop application (Java-based) |\n\nFor large volumes or less common objects, use Data Loader. For quick, guided imports of common objects, use Import Wizard."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj4_q2", number: 132, question: "External ID fields: how they enable upsert operations (insert + update) by matching incoming records to existing Salesforce records without knowing the Salesforce record ID", answer: "An External ID is a custom field flagged as an External ID (must be Text, Number, Email, or Auto-Number type). During an upsert operation:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj4_q3", number: 133, question: "Salesforce looks for an existing record where the External ID field matches the value in the import file.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj4_q4", number: 134, question: "If a match is found: the existing record is **updated**.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "data_analytics_obj4_q5", number: 135, question: "If no match is found: a new record is **inserted**.", answer: "This allows you to sync data from external systems using the external system's own identifier (e.g., an ERP customer ID) without needing to know Salesforce record IDs. External IDs can also be used to establish relationships between objects during import.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Environment Management and Deployment (2 objectives, 16 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "env_deploy",
            title: "Environment Management and Deployment",
            color: Color(red: 0.70, green: 0.30, blue: 0.40),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.98, green: 0.90, blue: 0.92),
            icon: "shippingbox.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "env_deploy_obj1",
                    title: "Describe the options available to move metadata between environments (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj1_q1", number: 136, question: "Sandbox types and selection criteria: Developer (5 MB data, no production licenses), Developer Pro (1 GB data), Partial Copy (5 GB, sample of production data, 5-day refresh), Full Copy (exact production replica, 29-day refresh minimum)", answer: "| Type | Storage | Data | Refresh Min. | Use Case |\n\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n| Developer | 5 MB data / 200 MB file | Metadata only | 1 day | Individual development, unit testing |\n\n| Developer Pro | 1 GB data / 1 GB file | Metadata only | 1 day | Larger dev/QA work |\n\n| Partial Copy | 5 GB data | Sample (via template) | 5 days | Integration testing with representative data |\n\n| Full Copy | Same as production | All production data | 29 days | UAT, compliance, performance testing |\n\nNo sandbox includes production usernames or active integrations — usernames are modified with a sandbox name suffix."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj1_q2", number: 137, question: "Sandbox refresh: what happens to existing sandbox data and metadata when a refresh is initiated, and whether customizations made in the sandbox since last refresh are preserved", answer: "When a sandbox is refreshed, the existing sandbox is completely overwritten by a fresh copy from production. All data and metadata in the sandbox (including customizations made since the last refresh) are permanently lost. This is irreversible. Before refreshing, export or document any sandbox-specific work you want to preserve. The refresh process takes the sandbox back to a clean state matching production at the moment the refresh runs."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj1_q3", number: 138, question: "Developer sandbox refresh interval: Developer and Developer Pro sandboxes have a 1-day minimum refresh interval, compared to Partial Copy (5 days) and Full Copy (29 days), and sandbox cloning as an alternative to refresh", answer: "- Developer / Developer Pro: Minimum 1-day refresh interval. Fastest to refresh.\n\n- Partial Copy: Minimum 5-day interval.\n\n- Full Copy: Minimum 29-day interval.\n\nSandbox cloning: Instead of refreshing from production, you can clone an existing sandbox to create a new sandbox copy of it. Useful for creating a snapshot of a sandbox at a specific point in time, branching work, or bypassing refresh interval restrictions. Cloning a sandbox counts against your sandbox license allocation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj1_q4", number: 139, question: "Sandbox templates for Partial Copy sandboxes: how a sandbox template specifies which objects' data to include in the partial copy and the limitation that templates cannot guarantee exact record counts", answer: "A sandbox template for a Partial Copy sandbox defines which objects' records should be included in the data copy (e.g., Accounts, Contacts, Cases, but not Order History). Salesforce copies a sample of records from each specified object, but the number of records is not guaranteed — Salesforce copies related records needed to maintain data integrity, which may include records from objects not in the template. Templates help keep the sandbox data relevant and reduce data volume, but they are not a precise data selection tool."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj1_q5", number: 140, question: "Change set deployment connections: authorizing a deployment connection between orgs requires a System Administrator in both the source and target org, and connections are unidirectional", answer: "Deployment connections between orgs (production to sandbox, sandbox to production, sandbox to sandbox) must be explicitly authorized. A System Administrator in the target org must log in and authorize the inbound connection. Connections are unidirectional: authorizing Org A to deploy to Org B does not automatically allow Org B to deploy to Org A. Each direction must be authorized separately. Without authorization, the outbound change set from the source org will not appear in the target org's inbound list."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj1_q6", number: 141, question: "Managed vs. unmanaged packages on AppExchange: managed packages have a namespace, can be upgraded, and have code hidden from subscribers; unmanaged packages expose all metadata and cannot be upgraded by the publisher", answer: "| | Managed Package | Unmanaged Package |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Namespace | Yes (unique prefix) | No |\n\n| Upgradeable | Yes — publisher pushes updates | No — each install is final |\n\n| Code visibility | Apex is hidden (protected) | All code is visible |\n\n| Subscriber deletion | Cannot delete managed components | Can delete anything |\n\n| Use case | ISV commercial products | Open-source templates, internal sharing |")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "env_deploy_obj2",
                    title: "Describe the capabilities and best practices for using change sets to move metadata between environments.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj2_q1", number: 142, question: "Inbound vs. outbound change sets: the workflow from creating an outbound change set in the source org, uploading it, and deploying the inbound change set in the target org, including the validation step", answer: "Workflow:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj2_q2", number: 143, question: "In the **source org**: Create an Outbound Change Set, add metadata components, and **Upload** it (sends it to connected target orgs).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj2_q3", number: 144, question: "In the **target org**: The uploaded change set appears as an **Inbound Change Set**.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj2_q4", number: 145, question: "In the target org: **Validate** the inbound change set (runs Apex tests, checks for errors without deploying — dry run).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj2_q5", number: 146, question: "If validation passes: **Deploy** the inbound change set to apply the metadata changes.", answer: "Always validate before deploying in production. Validation catches missing dependencies and test failures without making changes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj2_q6", number: 147, question: "Change set best practices for deployments: validating before deploying, the risk of deploying without running all tests in production, and the limitation that change sets cannot roll back a deployment", answer: "Best practices:\n\n- Always validate first in production before deploying.\n\n- Run all local tests during validation in production (required for production deployments with Apex).\n\n- Deploy during low-traffic windows to minimize user impact.\n\n- Document what is in each change set for rollback planning.\n\nRisk of skipping tests: If Apex code coverage drops below 75%, or if any test fails, the deployment fails mid-way, which can cause inconsistent metadata states.\n\nNo rollback: Change sets have no automated rollback mechanism. If a deployment causes issues, you must manually revert by deploying the previous version of metadata. Always have a rollback plan documented."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj2_q7", number: 148, question: "Deployment Status page: how to monitor the progress of an inbound change set deployment, view component-level success/failure details, and what the 'Quick Deploy' option requires (a validated change set that ran all tests within the last 10 days)", answer: "The Deployment Status page (Setup > Deploy > Deployment Status) shows:\n\n- In-progress deployments with a progress bar.\n\n- Completed and failed deployments.\n\n- Component-level detail: which components succeeded, which failed, and the specific error messages for failures.\n\n- Apex test results (pass/fail per test class).\n\nQuick Deploy: Once a change set is successfully validated with all tests passing, you can use Quick Deploy to deploy it to production without re-running tests. Quick Deploy is only available for 10 days after the validation. After 10 days, you must re-validate. This reduces deployment downtime in production."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj2_q8", number: 149, question: "Change set profile deployment behavior: profiles included in a change set are deployed incrementally — only the permissions explicitly in the changeset are deployed, not the full profile; permission sets deploy in full", answer: "When a profile is included in a change set, only the permissions related to the metadata components in that change set are deployed. The full profile is not overwritten. For example, if the change set contains a custom field, only the field-level security for that field from the profile is deployed. Existing profile settings for other components are untouched.\n\nPermission sets behave differently: they are deployed in full when included in a change set. All permissions in the permission set are applied, overwriting what is in the target org."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj2_q9", number: 150, question: "Metadata types excluded from change sets: which common configuration items CANNOT be included in change sets (e.g., Opportunity Stages, Lead Source picklist values, user records, documents) and must be migrated manually", answer: "Cannot be deployed via change sets:\n\n- Opportunity Stages and Lead Source picklist values (global value sets must be manually configured).\n\n- User records.\n\n- Documents, content, and attachments.\n\n- Chatter settings.\n\n- Certain Account Team and Territory settings.\n\n- Products and price books.\n\n- Business hours and holiday records.\n\nThese items must be manually recreated in the target org or handled via data migration tools (Data Loader, Import Wizard)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "env_deploy_obj2_q10", number: 151, question: "Automation behavior in sandboxes after refresh: flows and workflow rules that were active in production remain active in the sandbox after refresh; administrators must deactivate automation in sandbox to prevent unintended emails or record updates", answer: "After a sandbox refresh from production, all active automation (flows, workflow rules, process builder processes) from production is also active in the sandbox. This can cause:\n\n- Emails being sent to real users or customers from sandbox.\n\n- Record updates firing unexpectedly during sandbox testing.\n\n- Integration callouts to external systems.\n\nBest practice: Immediately after a sandbox refresh, deactivate or review all automation before performing any testing. Use deliverability settings (set email deliverability to \"System Email Only\" or \"No Access\") to prevent real emails from leaving the sandbox.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Process Automation (4 objectives, 50 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "process_automation",
            title: "Process Automation",
            color: Color(red: 0.40, green: 0.60, blue: 0.30),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.92, green: 0.96, blue: 0.90),
            icon: "gearshape.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "process_automation_obj1",
                    title: "Given a complex scenario, determine the solution using the best tool or combination of tools to solve a business problem.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q1", number: 152, question: "Record-Triggered Flow (before-save) vs. Record-Triggered Flow (after-save): a before-save flow updates the triggering record without a DML statement; an after-save flow can create/update related records but consumes DML governor limits", answer: "| | Before-Save | After-Save |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| When it runs | Before record is committed to DB | After record is committed to DB |\n\n| Update triggering record | Yes — no extra DML needed | Requires separate Update element (costs DML) |\n\n| Create/update other records | No | Yes |\n\n| DML usage | None for the triggering record | Yes — every create/update consumes DML |\n\n| Performance | Faster | Slightly slower |\n\nUse before-save when you only need to modify fields on the triggering record. Use after-save when you need to create or update other records."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q2", number: 153, question: "Schedule-Triggered Flow vs. after-save Flow with scheduled path: when a scheduled path on a record-triggered flow is preferable to a separate schedule-triggered flow for time-based actions", answer: "- Scheduled Path (on a Record-Triggered Flow): Best when the time-based action is relative to a record event (e.g., \"3 days after Close Date if Stage is still Open\"). The flow sets up the scheduled path at the time the record is saved.\n\n- Schedule-Triggered Flow: Best for batch processing on a fixed schedule (e.g., \"Every Monday at 9 AM, find all Cases opened more than 30 days ago and escalate them\"). Not tied to a specific record event.\n\nUse a scheduled path for time-sensitive follow-up on a per-record basis. Use a Schedule-Triggered Flow for periodic batch operations across many records."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q3", number: 154, question: "Approval process initial submission actions vs. final approval actions vs. final rejection actions vs. recall actions: which actions fire when and what field updates are allowed at each stage", answer: "| Stage | When It Fires | Allowed Actions |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Initial Submission | When user clicks Submit for Approval | Field updates, email alerts, tasks, outbound messages |\n\n| Final Approval | When all steps are approved | Field updates, email alerts, tasks, outbound messages, unlock record |\n\n| Final Rejection | When any step is rejected (and no reassignment) | Field updates, email alerts, tasks, outbound messages, unlock record |\n\n| Recall | When the submitter recalls the request | Field updates, email alerts, tasks |\n\nThe record is locked after submission and unlocked upon final approval, final rejection, or recall (if configured to unlock)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q4", number: 155, question: "Approval step approver types: related user (field-based lookup), queue, current user's manager (role hierarchy), designated approver — when to use each in a multi-step escalation scenario", answer: "- Related User (field-based): Approval goes to the user in a specified lookup field on the record (e.g., the Account Manager field). Use when the approver depends on the record's data.\n\n- Queue: Approval goes to a queue; any member can approve. Use for team-based approvals where any team member can act.\n\n- Current User's Manager (role hierarchy): Approval goes to the submitting user's manager as defined by the role hierarchy. Use for standard escalation/management-chain approvals.\n\n- Designated Approver: A specific named user or list of users always approves. Use for fixed approvals (e.g., always goes to Legal).\n\nMulti-step escalation commonly chains: submitter's manager → VP → C-level, using \"Current User's Manager\" for each step."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q5", number: 156, question: "Approval Processes vs. Record-Triggered Flows: when an approval process is the only declarative tool that can lock a record and require step-by-step human review with reassignment capability", answer: "An Approval Process is the only declarative tool that can:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q6", number: 157, question: "**Lock the record** against editing while pending review.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q7", number: 158, question: "Require **explicit human approval** at each step before proceeding.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q8", number: 159, question: "Allow **reassignment** of the approval to another user.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q9", number: 160, question: "Provide a structured **audit trail** of who approved/rejected and when.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q10", number: 161, question: "Notify **approvers** via email with approve/reject links.", answer: "A Record-Triggered Flow cannot lock records or create a structured human review workflow. If the business requirement involves multi-step human sign-off with record locking, an approval process is the correct tool."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q11", number: 162, question: "Approval process record locking: when a record is locked during an approval process, which user types can still edit it (admin, delegated approver), and how locking interacts with other automation", answer: "When a record is pending approval, it is locked from editing by standard users. The following can still edit it:\n\n- System Administrators (can always unlock or edit).\n\n- The designated approver (can edit if granted access in the approval process settings).\n\n- Delegated approvers (if reassigned to them).\n\nAutomation interaction: Even while locked, before-save and after-save flows triggered by other events can still update the record programmatically (the lock applies to UI edits, not all DML). Workflow field updates that fire as part of an approval action can also update locked records."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q12", number: 163, question: "Combining validation rules with Flow: a validation rule fires BEFORE an after-save flow but AFTER a before-save flow modifies field values; identifying the correct order to resolve a conflict between a validation rule and a before-save flow", answer: "Order on record save:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q13", number: 164, question: "Before-save flow runs (can set field values).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q14", number: 165, question: "Validation rules evaluate (they see the values set by the before-save flow).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q15", number: 166, question: "If validation passes, DML commits.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q16", number: 167, question: "After-save flow runs.", answer: "Conflict resolution: If a before-save flow sets Field A to a a validation rule to fail, fix the before-save flow logic so it sets an acceptable value. Do not try to fix this in the after-save flow, because validation runs before the after-save flow. If a validation rule is too strict for automation purposes, consider adding an exception in the validation rule formula (e.g., bypass when a hidden checkbox is set by the flow)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj1_q17", number: 168, question: "Autolaunched Flow invoked from a Process Builder: Process Builder is retired for new automation; the modern replacement is a Record-Triggered Flow or a scheduled path, and candidates must recognize retired Process Builder as a distractor", answer: "Process Builder is retired for new automation as of Salesforce's automation roadmap. Existing Process Builder processes still run, but you cannot create new ones. On the exam, if an answer choice involves Process Builder for a new use case, treat it as a distractor. The correct modern alternatives are:\n\n- Record-Triggered Flow (for record-based automation, including time-based actions via scheduled paths).\n\n- Schedule-Triggered Flow (for batch time-based automation).\n\n- Screen Flow (for user-interactive automation).")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "process_automation_obj2",
                    title: "Given a scenario, identify the appropriate tool or method for troubleshooting declarative automation.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj2_q1", number: 169, question: "Flow debug run: how the debug mode in Flow Builder simulates the flow execution, including the ability to set field values and roll back DML after debugging", answer: "In Flow Builder, click \"Debug\" to open the debug panel. You can:\n\n- Set the values of input variables (e.g., specify which record to process).\n\n- Step through the flow execution element by element, seeing the path taken and variable values at each step.\n\n- Choose to roll back DML after the debug run, meaning any records created or updated during the debug are automatically reverted. This allows safe testing in production without making real data changes.\n\nThe debug output shows each element executed, the outcome (which path was taken), and any errors encountered."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj2_q2", number: 170, question: "Debug logs for Flow: which log category and level must be set to trace flow execution (FLOW at FINE or FINER), and how to interpret the flow execution log entries", answer: "To capture Flow execution in debug logs:\n\n- Set the FLOW log category to FINE or FINER level when creating a debug log for a user (Setup > Debug Logs).\n\nIn the log, look for entries prefixed with `FLOW_`. Key entry types:\n\n- `FLOW_START_INTERVIEW_BEGIN` / `FLOW_START_INTERVIEW_END`: Flow interview started.\n\n- `FLOW_ELEMENT_BEGIN` / `FLOW_ELEMENT_END`: Each element's execution.\n\n- `FLOW_ELEMENT_ERROR`: An error occurred on a specific element.\n\n- `FLOW_INTERVIEW_FINISHED`: Flow completed successfully.\n\nTrace the element sequence and variable values to diagnose where the flow took an unexpected path or failed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj2_q3", number: 171, question: "Flow error emails: when Salesforce sends an email notification for a flow error, what information is included (element name, error message, flow version, running user), and how to suppress error emails for a specific flow", answer: "When a flow encounters an unhandled fault (no fault connector), Salesforce sends an email to the flow's creator and any system administrators. The email includes:\n\n- The flow name and version that errored.\n\n- The element name where the fault occurred.\n\n- The error message from Salesforce.\n\n- The running user (who triggered the flow).\n\n- A record link (if applicable) to the record being processed.\n\nTo suppress error emails: Add a Fault Path connector to the failing element. Route the fault to a graceful handling action (e.g., log a task, display a custom message). When a fault path exists, the error email is not sent."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj2_q4", number: 172, question: "Paused and failed flow interviews: how to monitor them in the Paused and Failed Flow Interviews list, and how an admin can resume or delete a paused interview", answer: "Paused flow interviews occur when a Screen Flow is paused by a user or when a flow hits a Pause element. Failed interviews occur when an unhandled error is caught.\n\nMonitor them in: Setup > Flow > Paused and Failed Flow Interviews.\n\nFrom this list, admins can:\n\n- Resume a paused interview (restarts execution from where it paused). Note: variables retain their values at pause time.\n\n- Delete a paused or failed interview (cancels the flow instance permanently).\n\nPaused interviews count against governor limits while they persist. Regularly monitor and clean up stale paused interviews.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "process_automation_obj3",
                    title: "Given a scenario, explain the capabilities and limitations of each declarative automation tool.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q1", number: 173, question: "Screen Flows vs. Autolaunched Flows: Screen Flows require user interaction and cannot be triggered by record changes; Autolaunched Flows run without a UI and can be invoked by other flows, processes, REST API, or Apex", answer: "| | Screen Flow | Autolaunched Flow |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| User interaction | Yes — has screens, input components | No — runs invisibly |\n\n| Triggered by record change | No (must be launched from a button, component, or Apex) | Yes — can be Record-Triggered |\n\n| Can be paused by user | Yes | No (can pause via Pause element) |\n\n| Invocation | Quick action, button, Lightning component, community | Other flows, REST API, Apex, Record-Trigger, Schedule-Trigger |\n\nScreen Flows are for guided user processes. Autolaunched Flows are for background automation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q2", number: 174, question: "Flow fault paths: how to add a Fault connector to a Flow element to gracefully handle errors (e.g., log a case record) instead of letting the flow error email fire", answer: "Every DML-producing element in a flow (Create Records, Update Records, Delete Records, Get Records that can fail, etc.) has a Fault connector output. To add a fault path: in Flow Builder, click the element and drag the Fault output to a handling element (e.g., a Create Records element that logs an error record, or a Screen element showing a friendly message).\n\nWith a fault path configured, when the element fails the flow follows the fault path instead of throwing an unhandled error. No error email is sent. The fault path gives you the `{!$Flow.FaultMessage}` variable containing the error text for logging."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q3", number: 175, question: "Flow bulkification: how Record-Triggered Flows automatically bulkify DML and SOQL operations within Get Records, Create Records, Update Records, and Delete Records elements; why using a loop with individual DML inside causes governor limit issues vs. collections-based DML outside the loop", answer: "Salesforce automatically bulkifies DML and SOQL operations in the standard flow elements (Get Records, Create Records, etc.) — meaning if a flow processes 200 records at once, all 200 Get Records calls are batched into one SOQL query.\n\nAnti-pattern: Placing a Create Records or Update Records element inside a Loop element causes one DML statement per loop iteration. For 200 records, that is 200 DML statements — quickly hitting the 150 DML limit per transaction.\n\nBest practice: Use Assignment elements inside the loop to build a collection variable. Then use a single Create/Update Records element outside the loop to perform one DML call for the entire collection."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q4", number: 176, question: "Flow loop limits: the maximum number of loop iterations in a single flow transaction (2,000) and what happens when the limit is exceeded (flow throws an error)", answer: "A single flow transaction can execute a maximum of 2,000 loop iterations across all Loop elements combined. If a loop exceeds this limit, the flow throws a runtime error and the transaction is rolled back. This is a hard platform limit. To avoid hitting it, design flows to work with smaller data sets, or use Batch Apex for large-volume processing that requires iterating over more records."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q5", number: 177, question: "Subflows: when to use a subflow (reusable logic called from a parent flow) vs. a standalone autolaunched flow, and whether subflows can be called from a before-save record-triggered flow", answer: "A Subflow element in a parent flow calls another flow and passes variables between them. Use a subflow when you have shared logic (e.g., a validation routine, a notification sequence) that is used by multiple parent flows. This promotes reuse and maintainability.\n\nBefore-save flow limitation: A before-save record-triggered flow cannot call a subflow (as of current platform constraints). Subflows can be called from after-save flows, screen flows, and other autolaunched flows. For before-save logic that is complex, the logic must live within the before-save flow itself."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q6", number: 178, question: "Assignment rules (Lead and Case): they fire after before-save flows and validation rules but before after-save flows; only one active assignment rule per object at a time", answer: "Lead and Case Assignment Rules fire after the record is committed to the database (after DML) but before after-save flows run. Specifically, they are part of the post-DML, pre-after-flow order of execution.\n\nKey constraints:\n\n- Only one active assignment rule per object (Lead or Case) at a time. If you need different routing logic, use multiple rule entries within the single active rule, ordered by priority.\n\n- Assignment rules are evaluated top-down by entry order; the first matching entry wins."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q7", number: 179, question: "Auto-response rules (Lead and Case): they fire in the same order-of-execution pass as assignment rules; only one active auto-response rule per object; they cannot trigger other automation", answer: "Auto-response rules automatically send an email to the lead or contact when a new Lead or Case is created (typically via web-to-case or web-to-lead). They fire in the same order-of-execution phase as assignment rules (post-DML). Only one auto-response rule can be active per object at a time. Auto-response rules send emails but do not trigger further automation (no flows, no workflow). They are purely for sending an acknowledgment email."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q8", number: 180, question: "Escalation rules (Case): time-based escalation that fires outside the record save transaction on a scheduled basis; does not re-fire if the case is modified after the escalation threshold is set", answer: "Escalation Rules fire on a scheduled basis (not during the record save transaction). When a case meets the entry criteria, Salesforce sets an escalation time threshold. A background job checks cases periodically and escalates those whose threshold has passed (e.g., reassign the case, send an email).\n\nImportant: Once the escalation threshold is set on a case, it does not automatically reset if the case is modified later. If a case is updated (e.g., status changes to Escalated), the escalation rule does not re-evaluate and re-fire from the updated state — it fires at the originally calculated time unless the case is closed or the rule's entry criteria no longer apply."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q9", number: 181, question: "Email alerts as automation actions: how a flow Email Alert action, an approval process email alert, and a workflow email alert all consume the same daily email limit (1,000 per standard user license per day, 2,000 per day for the org), and when that limit matters in automation design", answer: "All email alerts sent via automation (flows, approval processes, workflow rules) draw from the same daily email limit:\n\n- 1,000 emails per standard Salesforce user license per org per 24 hours.\n\n- Org-wide maximum of 2,000,000 emails per day (for larger orgs).\n\nThis limit matters when designing automation that sends emails in bulk (e.g., a flow that sends alerts for every record in a large batch import). If the limit is hit, emails stop sending and are lost (no queue). For high-volume email needs, use Marketing Cloud or a dedicated email service instead."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q10", number: 182, question: "Approval process limitations: cannot be triggered by a Flow directly (must use the Submit for Approval action), cannot have more than one active version at a time, and entry criteria evaluation options (always evaluate vs. evaluate only when specified fields change)", answer: "Key limitations:\n\n- A Flow cannot directly trigger an approval process via a standard element. You must use the \"Submit for Approval\" action in a flow or have the user click the Submit button. (Apex can invoke approvals programmatically.)\n\n- Only one active version of an approval process per object can exist at a time. Activating a new version deactivates the previous one.\n\n- Entry criteria evaluation: Two options — \"Evaluate the entry criteria based on the formula and the record meets the criteria\" (always evaluates) vs. \"Evaluate the entry criteria only when specified fields change.\" The latter improves performance by reducing unnecessary evaluations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj3_q11", number: 183, question: "Workflow Rules capabilities and retirement status: email alerts, field updates, outbound messages, tasks — and that Workflow Rules cannot be created for new automation, though existing rules still run", answer: "Workflow Rules support four action types:\n\n- Email Alert: Sends an email using an email template.\n\n- Field Update: Updates a field value on the same record (or parent, for master-detail).\n\n- Outbound Message: Sends XML data to an external endpoint via SOAP.\n\n- Task: Creates a task assigned to a user.\n\nRetirement status: Workflow Rules are retired for new automation in Salesforce as of Spring '23 for new customers and progressively for existing customers. You cannot create new Workflow Rules in new orgs, but existing rules continue to run. The modern replacement for all Workflow Rule use cases is a Record-Triggered Flow.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "process_automation_obj4",
                    title: "Understand the implications of the order of execution when using automation tools.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q1", number: 184, question: "Full Salesforce order of execution on record save: (1) before validation rules, (2) before-save flows, (3) standard field updates (required fields), (4) duplicate rules, (5) DML to database, (6) assignment/auto-response/escalation rules, (7) workflow field updates (re-runs validation), (8) processes and after-save flows, (9) post-commit logic (email alerts, async jobs)", answer: "Full order on record save (simplified for exam purposes):"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q2", number: 185, question: "System validations (required fields, unique fields, field format).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q3", number: 186, question: "**Before-save Record-Triggered Flows**.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q4", number: 187, question: "Custom validation rules.", answer: "4. Duplicate rules."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q5", number: 188, question: "**Commit to database (DML)**.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q6", number: 189, question: "Assignment rules, auto-response rules.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q7", number: 190, question: "Workflow rules and field updates (field updates cause validation rules to re-run).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q8", number: 191, question: "**After-save Record-Triggered Flows** and processes.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q9", number: 192, question: "Post-commit actions: email alerts, async jobs, platform events.", answer: "Memorize this sequence — exam scenarios often test which automation fires before or after another."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q10", number: 193, question: "Before-save Flow position in order of execution: it fires AFTER system validation (required fields, unique fields) but BEFORE DML, meaning it can modify field values that will be committed in the same DML operation without consuming a DML statement", answer: "The before-save flow runs after Salesforce checks required fields and uniqueness (system validation), but before the record is committed to the database. Because the record has not yet been saved, any field modifications made by the before-save flow are included in the original DML commit — no additional DML statement is consumed. This makes before-save flows highly efficient for setting default values, auto-calculating fields, or normalizing data before save."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q11", number: 194, question: "After-save Flow vs. before-save Flow in a multi-trigger scenario: given a record save that fires both a before-save flow and an after-save flow, identifying which fields are visible to each flow during its execution context", answer: "- Before-save flow: Runs before DML. It sees the new field values being saved (including values from the triggering change), but the record is not yet in the database. The `$Record` variable holds the new values; `$Record__Prior` holds the previous values.\n\n- After-save flow: Runs after DML. The record is committed. It can query the record's new state or related records. Changes made to the triggering record by the before-save flow are visible to the after-save flow (they were committed in DML). Related records can be queried or updated."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q12", number: 195, question: "Workflow Rule field update re-trigger: when a workflow field update fires, it causes validation rules to re-run on the record; if the field update changes a value that a validation rule evaluates, the validation rule may now fail", answer: "When a Workflow Rule field update fires, it triggers a second pass of validation rules on the record (to ensure data integrity after the field update). If the workflow field update changes a field that is evaluated in a validation rule, and the new value causes the validation rule to evaluate to `TRUE` (error condition), the save fails — even though the original record data was valid. This can create unexpected save failures. The fix is typically to update the validation rule formula to account for the state set by the workflow, or to move the logic to a flow."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q13", number: 196, question: "Multiple after-save flows on the same object and trigger context: the order in which multiple after-save record-triggered flows execute (alphabetical by flow label within the same trigger context) and how to control execution order", answer: "When multiple after-save Record-Triggered Flows exist on the same object with the same trigger context (e.g., both fire on record creation), Salesforce executes them in alphabetical order by flow label (API name of the flow). This is not configurable in the UI.\n\nTo control execution order: Prefix flow labels with numbers (e.g., \"01 - Create Related Task,\" \"02 - Send Notification Email\"). This ensures a predictable alphabetical sort. Alternatively, consolidate related automation into a single flow with decision elements, giving you full control over sequencing."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q14", number: 197, question: "Recursive flow prevention: how to prevent an after-save flow from triggering itself infinitely when it updates the same record, including the use of isChanged() and $Record__Prior variables", answer: "An after-save flow that updates the same record it was triggered by will re-trigger itself (since the update causes another save event). To prevent infinite loops:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q15", number: 198, question: "**Use `ISCHANGED()` conditions**: Add an entry condition to the flow that only runs when a specific field has changed from a specific value. If the flow's update doesn't change that field, it won't re-trigger.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q16", number: 199, question: "**Use `$Record__Prior`**: Compare current and prior values to detect whether the triggering change is the one you care about (e.g., only proceed if Status changed from \"Open\" to \"Closed\" — not from the value your flow sets).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q17", number: 200, question: "**Use a \"processed\" flag**: Set a checkbox field that the flow also checks — if the flag is already set, the flow exits immediately, preventing further processing.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "process_automation_obj4_q18", number: 201, question: "When declarative automation cannot satisfy the requirement: identifying scenarios (e.g., complex cross-object field rollups on lookup relationships, real-time integration callouts, recursive logic requiring loop control) that require Apex code rather than declarative tools", answer: "Declarative tools (Flow, Approval Processes, etc.) have limits. Escalate to Apex code when the requirement involves:\n\n- Roll-up calculations on lookup relationships (Flow can query related records but cannot replicate true roll-up summary behavior on lookups at scale).\n\n- Real-time synchronous callouts to external systems during a record save (Flows can use External Services for some callouts, but complex/custom HTTP callouts require Apex).\n\n- Complex recursive logic requiring precise loop control beyond Flow's 2,000-iteration limit.\n\n- Executing logic before a flow's capabilities (e.g., before-insert batch processing in Apex triggers).\n\n- Cross-transaction state management using static variables to prevent recursion in complex multi-object scenarios.\n\n- Transforming large data sets in memory with nested loops and branching logic that would exceed flow limits.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        )

    ]
}
